=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ $\\,x=1,\\,$ and $\\,x=3,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}-2(-1)^{2})=-3{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}-2(1)^{2})=-e<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{3}((3)^{3}-2(3)^{2})=9{e}^{3}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2,$ we conclude that $x=2$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only $x=2$ is an inflection point." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186100823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250162 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186100823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250163 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186100999 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250404 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186101000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250406 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186105716 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250121 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186105820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250481 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186105820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250482 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186105821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250474 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186110721 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250343 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186110721 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250344 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186110722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250345 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186110723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250363 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186110723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250367 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186110724 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250372 13194139533395 