86045585 true] [17592186166867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250543 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186166867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250547 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186167089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163582 13194139533395 17592186045585 false] [17592186167089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249855 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186167089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186167089 1074 "![](images/1e/1e37d101f131279fc198fd97f03e6a35e15bc2d62622e1730aadbc316dc8d0bf.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}(y+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186167094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163612 13194139533395 17592186045585 false] [17592186167094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249917 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186167094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186167094 1071 "![](images/66/660feebe44e179b53be85d23c497e58de4774258b588a6f8ac785a0fd9c8fc08.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2y-{y}^{2}-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{3}{y-1}$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186167094 1071 "![](images/66/660feebe44e179b53be85d23c497e58de4774258b588a6f8ac785a0fd9c8fc08.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{2y - y^{2} - 1}$\n2. $y^{′} =-\\frac{y^{2} + y}{y - 1}$\n3. $y^{′} = \\frac{3}{y - 1}$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 false] [17592186167094 1074 "![](images/a7/a76856ea0a6b50b821ba6c39e6a87a5acf5aabd704454b4875540b3970f3d249.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are negative everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2y-{y}^{2}-1}=-\\frac{1}{(y-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 1.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is negative for all $y\\ne 1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}=-\\frac{y+1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to ∓\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{3}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for $y>1.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186171966 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250491 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186171967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250521 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [175921