e] [17592186250119 1075 #edb/ref 17592186248853 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250119 1075 #edb/ref 17592186249004 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250119 1075 #edb/ref 17592186249257 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250119 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250119 1077 "selection" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250119 1079 #edb/ref 17592186249257 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250119 1080 #uuid "faa7acea-63e1-5f4b-ad99-c211d52b1202" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250120 1075 #edb/ref 17592186247431 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250120 1075 #edb/ref 17592186247655 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250120 1075 #edb/ref 17592186247954 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250120 1075 #edb/ref 17592186248312 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250120 1075 #edb/ref 17592186249643 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250120 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250120 1077 "selection" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250120 1079 #edb/ref 17592186249643 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250120 1080 #uuid "fea7e416-284b-5883-9657-5298570ef502" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249871 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250121 1069 "q-113768" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250121 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250121 1071 "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{{x}^{3}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250121 1072 #uuid "12fa2cf8-ca3e-539b-acee-bcc3ab458a7a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250121 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(2x-3){x}^{3}-3({x}^{2}-3x+3){x}^{2}}{{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-3{x}^{2}+9x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+6x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nSo, $x=3$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $(0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{(1-3)^{2}}{{1}^{4}}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =-\\frac{(4-3)^{2}}{{4}^{4}}=-\\frac{1}{256}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=3.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=3$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema on $(0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250059 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250122 1069 "q-113769" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250122 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250122 1071 "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}$ on the closed interval $[-1,1].$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250122 1072 #uuid "dd5b5512-78fa-5dfa-824a-d1dc0966954f" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250122 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2(x+1){e}^{x}-(x+1)^{2}({e}^{x})}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+2-{x}^{2}-2x-1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-x)(1+x)}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-1,1,$ which coincide with the endpoints of the given interval.\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =\\frac{(-1+1)^{2}}{{e}^{-1}}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{(1+1)^{2}}{{e}^{1}}=\\frac{4}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=\\frac{4}{e},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{4}{e}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-1)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](images/e2/e21d39f09f56fa1113184ce7a778d98561b82b932cd324ee90cf3aaff4d729d9.png)" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249721 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250123 1069 "q-113818" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250123 1071 "$\\frac{5}{h}÷\\frac{10}{k}=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250123 1072 #uuid "d696dd66-1554-57e6-9d74-9c8f4a560ef5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250123 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{h}÷\\frac{10}{k},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{h}÷\\frac{10}{k} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{h}\\cdot \\frac{k}{10} & = \\\\ \\frac{5k}{10h} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot k}{\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2h} & = \\\\ \\frac{k}{2h} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $5$ in the numerator and denominator." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249963 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250124 1069 "q-113821" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250124 1071 "What is $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}z}÷\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{6{z}^{3}}?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250124 1072 #uuid "73d1b02b-baff-5504-b510-6aee5b6536db" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250124 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}z}÷\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{6{z}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}z}÷\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{6{z}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}z}\\cdot \\frac{6{z}^{3}}{7{x}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}\\cdot 2z}\\cdot \\frac{3\\cdot 2z\\cdot {z}^{2}}{7\\cdot {x}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{{y}^{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2z}}\\cdot \\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{2z}\\cdot {z}^{2}}{7\\cdot \\cancel{{x}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3{z}^{2}}{7{y}^{2}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250028 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250125 1069 "q-113824" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250125 1071 "Given that $\\frac{4{g}^{2}}{7{h}^{3}}=a$ and $\\frac{12g}{7h}=b,$ what is $a÷b?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250125 1072 #uuid "ba32a502-c504-5632-a2b1-6335fbd8b6b5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250125 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\na÷b=\\frac{4{g}^{2}}{7{h}^{3}}÷\\frac{12g}{7h},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{g}^{2}}{7{h}^{3}}÷\\frac{12g}{7h} & = \\\\ \\frac{4{g}^{2}}{7{h}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{7h}{12g} & = \\\\ \\frac{4g\\cdot g}{7h\\cdot {h}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{7h}{3\\cdot 4g} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4g}\\cdot g}{\\cancel{7h}\\cdot {h}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{7h}}{3\\cdot \\cancel{4g}} & = \\\\ \\frac{g}{3{h}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249945 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250126 1069 "q-113826" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250126 1071 "Given that $\\frac{3(z-2)}{(z+1)^{2}}=a$ and $\\frac{z-2}{6(z+1)}=b,$ what is $a÷b?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250126 1072 #uuid "fd3f875b-33f7-55de-93e3-693ddb1d2606" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250126 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\na÷b=\\frac{3(z-2)}{(z+1)^{2}}÷\\frac{z-2}{6(z+1)},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(z-2)}{(z+1)^{2}}÷\\frac{z-2}{6(z+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(z-2)}{(z+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{6(z+1)}{z-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cancel{(z-2)}}{(z+1)^{\\cancel{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{6\\cancel{(z+1)}}{\\cancel{(z-2)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{z+1}\\cdot 6 & = \\\\ \\frac{18}{z+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250001 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250127 1069 "q-113827" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250127 1071 "Consider the following polynomial division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5x-3 \\\\  & x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,5{x}^{2}+2x-6\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{5{x}^{2}+5x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3x-6 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-3x-3}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, $\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x-6}{x+1}=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250127 1072 #uuid "78ade1c1-b8d2-54a1-863a-67f576c1f1f7" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250127 1074 "$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5x-3 \\\\  & x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,5{x}^{2}+2x-6\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{5{x}^{2}+5x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3x-6 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-3x-3}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFrom the given polynomial division, we deduce that dividing the polynomial $5{x}^{2}+2x-6$ by the linear binomial $x+1$ gives\n\n- the quotient $5x-3,$ and\n\n- the remainder $-3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x-6}{x+1}=5x-3-\\frac{3}{x+1}.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249817 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250128 1069 "q-113833" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250128 1071 "Calculate $\\int {e}^{x/2\\,-\\,1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250128 1072 #uuid "0dd276ae-ab9b-5d13-9781-196a361b49ac" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250128 1074 "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{2}-1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,2\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{x/2\\,-\\,1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\,{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =2\\,{e}^{x/2\\,-\\,1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249975 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250129 1069 "q-113847" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250129 1071 "$\\int 8{x}^{3}{e}^{4-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250129 1072 #uuid "cea6cfb7-b02b-5433-ac98-e8175ace7d35" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250129 1074 "Let's introduce the new variable $u=4-{x}^{4}.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,-2\\text{d}u=8{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8{x}^{3}{e}^{4-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{4-{x}^{4}}\\cdot 8{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot (-2)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =-2{e}^{4-{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250002 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250130 1069 "q-113848" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250130 1071 "Find the antiderivative of \n$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}+{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250130 1072 #uuid "e132caee-594d-5f81-b6a1-2e2a44a2d5f6" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250130 1074 "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}+{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/3+1}}{(-\\frac{4}{3}+1)}+\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{(3+1)}-\\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/3}}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{(-1)}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{1}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249739 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250131 1069 "q-113853" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250131 1071 "$\\int -4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250131 1072 #uuid "6434d2e4-0465-5195-adb8-8688c42e1ab0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250131 1074 "Factoring out the constant $-4$ and calculating the resulting integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-4\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249766 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250132 1069 "q-113868" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250132 1071 "![](images/14/149fd953075c1d9909e036d4935ccd4f17ad1734d86bba4a70254468cd2035d5.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x+2$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250132 1072 #uuid "63bcbbf8-c502-5d3b-bd0e-da913f8e74d5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250132 1074 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x+2$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249720 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250133 1069 "q-113873" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250133 1071 "Calculate the integral$\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250133 1072 #uuid "e2191fab-edb1-5e72-a762-ef056bc648a3" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250133 1074 "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u={e}^{2x}-2x.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}-2=2({e}^{2x}-1)\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=({e}^{2x}-1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}-2x}\\cdot ({e}^{2x}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{e}^{2x}-2x|}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249981 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250134 1069 "q-113881" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250134 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250134 1072 #uuid "7115006d-07e5-5f15-ba88-d56671b9f015" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250134 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=0,$ and the upper bound is $b=1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{n}=\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249878 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250135 1069 "q-113884" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250135 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250135 1072 #uuid "35148af9-fe39-53be-bd54-85b1a5b3aea3" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250135 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={e}^{x-1},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{1+4i/n-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{4i/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249788 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250136 1069 "q-113887" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250136 1071 "Calculate $\\int {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250136 1072 #uuid "333b8bc2-8d50-5f18-bbc4-8fbfe08b346d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250136 1074 "To compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249929 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250137 1069 "q-113890" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250137 1071 "$\\int 2\\cdot {9}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250137 1072 #uuid "763936f6-7387-5f00-bda0-da6f24c0d625" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250137 1074 "First, we factor out the constant $2.$ Then, we integrate the exponential function and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\cdot {9}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {9}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{9}^{x}}{ln⁡9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249926 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250138 1069 "q-113893" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250138 1071 "Calculate $\\int {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250138 1072 #uuid "89cd3b1f-2f20-5813-8f46-6c4d05bd7fcb" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250138 1074 "To compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}}{ln⁡(\\frac{1}{8})}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the laws of logarithms, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ln⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =ln⁡1-ln⁡8 \\\\  & =0-ln⁡8 \\\\  & =-ln⁡8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify our final answer as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}}{ln⁡(\\frac{1}{8})}+C=-\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}}{ln⁡8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249890 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250139 1069 "q-113899" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250139 1071 "![](images/42/42fd32f22d25ed1d26d48ca8d4f1f5f454bcca67c68febaec066fa3b8bde0783.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* ${f}^{′}(x)<0?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250139 1072 #uuid "6933ec55-a667-58bc-9cb1-16c146a2c5d3" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250139 1074 "We begin by noticing that at $x=a$ the graph has a corner point, which implies that the derivative doesn't exist there.\n\nThe derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is negative when $f(x)$ is decreasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=c$ are on decreasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](images/f6/f6e6c7b869268eba113690776b0aa07713ff12adb675e22e11406c4a13f0e7b2.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=c,$ the graph is concave down ($⌢$) at $x=c$ only." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250049 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250140 1069 "q-113907" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250140 1071 "$\\int (-6sin⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250140 1072 #uuid "4099e9a0-92e6-5376-9bf3-b36d248db1d1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250140 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int sin⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =-cos⁡\\theta +C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (-6sin⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =-6\\int sin⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-6(-cos⁡\\theta )+C \\\\  & =6cos⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249781 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250141 1069 "q-113908" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250141 1071 "$\\int 5cos⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250141 1072 #uuid "51e2c13f-c606-5d86-a345-77ece48f02ef" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250141 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int cos⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=sin⁡y+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 5cos⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =5\\int cos⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =5(sin⁡y)+C \\\\  & =5sin⁡y+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249757 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250142 1069 "q-113909" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250142 1071 "$\\int (2cos⁡x+3sin⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250142 1072 #uuid "024b6258-39f4-52fd-a000-550a30db6b19" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250142 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-cos⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int cos⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =sin⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2cos⁡x+3sin⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int cos⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2sin⁡x+3(-cos⁡x)+C \\\\  & =2sin⁡x-3cos⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250013 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250143 1069 "q-113917" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250143 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250143 1072 #uuid "4f080615-a4c5-5d2c-a5e1-27e9c5e2295d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250143 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=\\frac{2k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{k}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $b=2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250012 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250144 1069 "q-113920" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250144 1071 "$\\int -3csc⁡x\\,cot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250144 1072 #uuid "0ba0c93b-e27b-584f-9b8e-5ad2b653f757" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250144 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-csc⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -3csc⁡x\\,cot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3(-csc⁡x)+C \\\\  & =3csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249968 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250145 1069 "q-113926" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250145 1071 "$\\int (4sin⁡x-3csc⁡x\\,cot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250145 1072 #uuid "2dc9cd23-747b-56ac-b00a-28a17c09b555" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250145 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-cos⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4sin⁡x-3csc⁡x\\,cot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int csc⁡x\\,cot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(-cos⁡x)-3(-csc⁡x)+C \\\\  & =-4cos⁡x+3csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249941 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250146 1069 "q-113928" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250146 1071 "$\\int (\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250146 1072 #uuid "074a2e50-f7c4-5107-9924-a9071d84d3a8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250146 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249931 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250147 1069 "q-113930" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250147 1071 "$\\int (3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+7\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250147 1072 #uuid "cfe374fe-d98b-50a6-a8d1-5eccc1ef91ef" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250147 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+7\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+7(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-7\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249687 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250148 1069 "q-113936" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250148 1071 "$\\int 2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250148 1072 #uuid "1eae0c2a-9fbb-58f9-9e02-1ea035b71ceb" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250148 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =tan⁡\\theta +C\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =2\\int {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2tan⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249942 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250149 1069 "q-113937" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250149 1071 "$\\int -4{sec}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250149 1072 #uuid "09d61645-0832-5f0f-8786-3d306da259b5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250149 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {sec}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z=tan⁡z+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -4{sec}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z & =-4\\int {sec}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-4(tan⁡z)+C \\\\  & =-4tan⁡z+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249695 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250150 1069 "q-113944" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250150 1071 "$\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250150 1072 #uuid "08d0f1cc-d4ff-5f13-b460-2feb29c2a12c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250150 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249948 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250151 1069 "q-113947" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250151 1071 "$\\int 5{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250151 1072 #uuid "8f660826-f465-5ee2-b41b-5a557d761074" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250151 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =-cot⁡\\theta +C\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =5\\int {csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5(-cot⁡\\theta )+C \\\\  & =-5cot⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249900 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250152 1069 "q-113983" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250152 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x+1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250152 1072 #uuid "9801153f-b480-580e-a696-7b55c194e174" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250152 1074 "If we let $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x+1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({u}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x}{3}+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249725 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250153 1069 "q-113989" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250153 1071 "${\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250153 1072 #uuid "e8ba6724-aa4a-53a8-8ea0-e040d768a286" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250153 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{(-4+1)}{|}_{-3}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3}}{(-3)}{|}_{-3}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}{|}_{-3}^{-1} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{3(-1)^{3}}-\\frac{1}{3(-3)^{3}}) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{81}) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{26}{81}) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250060 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250154 1069 "q-113998" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250154 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250154 1072 #uuid "cf1ad1f8-f4e6-5785-9ed6-ccc3fe200091" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250154 1074 "Let $u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2}\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249826 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250155 1069 "q-114006" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250155 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250155 1072 #uuid "e00da540-5f89-5bb0-be7b-7af0ae92a04e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250155 1074 "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250114 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250156 1069 "q-114007" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250156 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{3}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250156 1072 #uuid "225b3ea5-bb8d-54c8-bae9-8867b13798a5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250156 1074 "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249743 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250157 1069 "q-114008" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250157 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{-1}^{-1}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250157 1072 #uuid "8b3c015d-f259-58eb-969a-e0501ef667f4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250157 1074 "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249868 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250158 1069 "q-114015" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250158 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}(f(x)-5)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250158 1072 #uuid "351406a9-733f-57ff-944a-fe8cfe0f2f36" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250158 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(f(x)-5)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-5\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1-5(2-0) \\\\  & =1-5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249696 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250159 1069 "q-114016" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250159 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-6,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}(8-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250159 1072 #uuid "6fd8a7bc-87ff-5981-8dda-3fdd4a286792" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250159 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(8-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =8{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\cdot x\\,{|}_{1}^{3}-6 \\\\  & =8(3-1)-6 \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2-6 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249909 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250160 1069 "q-114017" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250160 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250160 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}(7-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250160 1072 #uuid "7cdf8ecb-2bec-5462-bd81-f7274fc69361" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250160 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(7-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =7{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot x\\,{|}_{2}^{4}-5\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =7(4-2)+20 \\\\  & =7\\cdot 2+20 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249799 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250161 1069 "q-114019" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250161 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}(f(x)-6x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250161 1072 #uuid "78c51f41-fb2a-59de-87f9-0bb3b871e751" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250161 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(f(x)-6x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =1-3({1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =1-3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249876 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250162 1069 "q-114020" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250162 1071 "$\\int \\frac{3{log}_{6}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250162 1072 #uuid "5c740bb4-ff2e-5c3c-8f3d-5a68fa36c853" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250162 1074 "Let $u={log}_{6}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{log}_{6}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int ({log}_{6}⁡x)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =3\\int {u}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\int {u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\cdot {log}_{6}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249749 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250163 1069 "q-114021" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250163 1071 "$\\int \\frac{{log}_{7}⁡x}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250163 1072 #uuid "1cd00feb-cfab-542a-aa56-7db9c40f1132" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250163 1074 "Let $u={log}_{7}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{7}⁡x}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {log}_{7}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{2}\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{4}\\cdot {log}_{7}^{2}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249998 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250164 1069 "q-114022" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250164 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250164 1072 #uuid "b2f614f9-7ab2-5976-a519-b4171e38a53c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250164 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $25{x}^{2}=(5x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(5x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250039 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250165 1069 "q-114023" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250165 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250165 1072 #uuid "49cf1e17-5602-5d73-b695-bcf78c6bba28" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250165 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $36{x}^{2}=(6x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=6x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(6x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249857 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250166 1069 "q-114024" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250166 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250166 1072 #uuid "0f4bd8aa-6ac8-5557-b597-a73e9dcdaedc" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250166 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $2{x}^{2}=(\\sqrt{2}x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{2}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(\\sqrt{2}x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249997 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250167 1069 "q-114025" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250167 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{1-81{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250167 1072 #uuid "8d5a14b8-efd2-53f8-9736-e9a0e599835e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250167 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $81{x}^{2}=(9x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=9x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{1-81{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(9x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(9x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249730 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250168 1069 "q-114026" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250168 1071 "$\\int \\frac{-2}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250168 1072 #uuid "620c9fa2-b98d-514c-8603-da573e96fa60" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250168 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-2}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249980 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250169 1069 "q-114027" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250169 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250169 1072 #uuid "836b44bd-5d8f-5551-957e-758fcb5e8836" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250169 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $3{x}^{2}=(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{3}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{1-(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250116 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250170 1069 "q-114030" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250170 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250170 1072 #uuid "e7c70ecc-510e-5481-bfab-ec3f7c63aab2" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250170 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/25)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/25}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$ \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get  \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (5\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249911 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250171 1069 "q-114032" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250171 107