ac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{7\\sqrt{7}}{|\\sqrt{7}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{7}x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\sqrt{7}x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{7}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{7}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{7{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{7\\sqrt{7}}{|\\sqrt{7}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{7}x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\sqrt{7}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =7\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{7}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250096 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250184 1069 "q-114056" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250184 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{8}{|x|\\sqrt{8{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250184 1072 #uuid "d978df6a-8484-5ed1-885e-b4758185514d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250184 1074 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{8}{|x|\\sqrt{8{x}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{8\\sqrt{8}}{|\\sqrt{8}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{8}x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\sqrt{8}x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{8}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{8}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{|x|\\sqrt{8{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8\\sqrt{8}}{|\\sqrt{8}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{8}x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{8}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{8}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249708 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250185 1069 "q-114057" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250185 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{100{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250185 1072 #uuid "1386e5e4-c2d1-50fc-ab2e-40d086cf4081" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250185 1074 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{100{x}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{50}{|10x|\\sqrt{(10x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $10x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=10x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{100{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{50}{|10x|\\sqrt{(10x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =50\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{arcsec}(10x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250022 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250186 1069 "q-114058" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250186 1071 "![](images/2c/2c9a731594d1c6a781543b5e5fad5b14e7905848ccf1d695cf2019bc931a8e78.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[0,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250186 1072 #uuid "c0c20158-ed7a-5907-95b8-7188f2ee33e1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250186 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upwards like a smile ($⌣$) over the interval $(2,3).$\n\n![](images/ec/ece77074a3d712b76ed55150910382ed5c0bb1ddf7d565bc2d2200a72394c4b1.png)" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249839 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250187 1069 "q-114060" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250187 1071 "![](images/c5/c528a8bdbb9bc6dd1a9895109fdc6818b70787c5024c78e06e2d2ec3997777d6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250187 1072 #uuid "ea7eabda-14a0-5474-9217-5f81cc639ac5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250187 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals  $(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1).$  Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the intervals  $(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249737 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250188 1069 "q-114061" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250188 1071 "![](images/d3/d3bd98f5662140655dd2bd33f99dc8704b1bd03b6004133dc8492ac51ea3a347.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={h}^{″}(x).$  Find the intervals on which the function $h(x)$ is concave down." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250188 1072 #uuid "46bcfc60-8725-59cd-b8fd-64ca9ea00d0a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250188 1074 "A function $h(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${h}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={h}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${h}^{″}(x)<0$ on the intervals $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,4).$  Therefore, the function $h(x)$ is concave down on the intervals $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249952 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250189 1069 "q-114063" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250189 1071 "![](images/9a/9ac6bf6978169aaa3a29c570234cdbf26f37b6e91488d8e27c63255b8cbb3ba6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x),$ where the function $f(x)$ is defined on $[0,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250189 1072 #uuid "29b9af77-f2b2-5014-8fef-77e6f7161080" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250189 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ on the interval  $(1,2)$ only. Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(1,2)$ only." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249852 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250190 1069 "q-114074" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250190 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250190 1072 #uuid "b4d360a8-fe79-5362-9972-fed2a1994303" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250190 1074 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249744 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250191 1069 "q-114078" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250191 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250191 1072 #uuid "d8425d47-32c7-547c-ab87-ac1c6071e1e8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250191 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/8)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/8}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{8})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{8}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{8}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{8}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{8})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{8}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250110 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250192 1069 "q-114080" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250192 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250192 1072 #uuid "c216ad6d-32f4-5235-ae6f-6f4e1e6db054" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250192 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/4)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249736 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250193 1069 "q-114084" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250193 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250193 1072 #uuid "d206bd2a-2b8b-589e-945a-46c1dd7ed6d1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250193 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/3)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249724 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250194 1069 "q-11410" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250194 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)={(5x+10)}^{6}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250194 1072 #uuid "421599a5-1cf3-595e-9d3b-cce9b649fa80" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250194 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{6},\\,g=5x+10\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6{g}^{5},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =30(5x+10{)}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249691 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250195 1069 "q-114133" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250195 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3,$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250195 1072 #uuid "09cb1fe0-ddf5-5295-9435-8a5ac4ba0a1d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250195 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+3 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 1 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1+2(3-0) \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249949 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250196 1069 "q-114135" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250196 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4,$ then what is ${\\int }_{3}^{6}(5-2f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250196 1072 #uuid "5bcf812a-16bf-5de3-9c19-83208c1eff9c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250196 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8+4 & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 12 & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}(5-2f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot x\\,{|}_{3}^{6}-2\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =5(6-3)-24 \\\\  & =5(3)-24 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249932 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250197 1069 "q-114138" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250197 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1,$ then what is ${\\int }_{-1}^{3}(2f(x)-3)\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250197 1072 #uuid "7dc2a03b-684b-5f0e-9c85-5812f2348f6c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250197 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+1 & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 3 & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(2f(x)-3)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-3\\cdot x\\,{|}_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-3(3-(-1)) \\\\  & =6-3(4) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249772 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250198 1069 "q-114143" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250198 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{8}^{5}3f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250198 1072 #uuid "ee7add6e-880a-5bf1-914a-15e4c6c7c466" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250198 1074 "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{8}^{5}3f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ 3{\\int }_{8}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ -3{\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ {\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{5}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249704 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250199 1069 "q-114153" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250199 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{4}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ and ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250199 1072 #uuid "deaa32f0-67ca-5a9c-a850-d214c4a09844" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250199 1074 "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6+(-6) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250008 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250200 1069 "q-114156" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250200 1071 "$\\int (x+\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250200 1072 #uuid "2a79dcdc-c1c8-5a71-9d84-cbbd212d39b8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250200 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249688 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250201 1069 "q-114160" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250201 1071 "$\\int -\\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250201 1072 #uuid "91544b16-e52d-5608-9076-7f8f50d9687c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250201 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-5\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5arcsecx+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249694 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250202 1069 "q-114162" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250202 1071 "$\\int -\\frac{12}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250202 1072 #uuid "337f64c2-ac77-50d2-b155-e1f75e2f921e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250202 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{12}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-12\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-12arcsecx+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249918 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250203 1069 "q-114164" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250203 1071 "$\\int (3{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250203 1072 #uuid "eb0e1654-81f3-51ff-a1ad-22ecef08f60d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250203 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{7}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+7(\\text{ arcsec }x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+7\\text{ arcsec }x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249935 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250204 1069 "q-114168" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250204 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250204 1072 #uuid "ca385184-ef23-5e09-850b-a334c3c5908a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250204 1074 "Factoring the constant out of the integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =3(0-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249838 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250205 1069 "q-114173" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250205 1071 "${\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{t}{8}\\,\\text{d}t=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250205 1072 #uuid "7c16fd79-a975-5b75-bbad-7fc7af9d9c70" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250205 1074 "Factoring the constant out of the integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{t}{8}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{2}^{3}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}({3}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(9-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249815 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250206 1069 "q-114175" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250206 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250206 1072 #uuid "e4ab15c5-bd19-5639-9b61-2d4a68d67bce" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250206 1074 "Splitting up the integral into the difference of two integrals and evaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{2}-{1}^{2})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3)+(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249865 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250207 1069 "q-114181" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250207 1071 "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250207 1072 #uuid "9199096c-1056-59b2-bc8e-bf89ee34e2ca" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250207 1074 "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-1+6}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)+5}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+5\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249872 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250208 1069 "q-114183" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250208 1071 "$\\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250208 1072 #uuid "5d5fd5d6-3989-51af-a4dc-33f4a2f20e44" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250208 1074 "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}+5-5}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5({x}^{2}+1)-5}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cancel{({x}^{2}+1)}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (5-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5x-5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250015 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250209 1069 "q-114184" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250209 1071 "$\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250209 1072 #uuid "7663d90a-9599-5f84-ad9c-e23f99291791" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250209 1074 "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}+4-4}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4({x}^{2}+1)-4}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{({x}^{2}+1)}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250083 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250210 1069 "q-114190" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250210 1071 "${\\int }_{-2}^{1}(6{z}^{2}-5{z}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250210 1072 #uuid "cb7aa667-7846-593b-9726-bcbf9d59d176" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250210 1074 "Splitting up the difference and factoring out the constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(6{z}^{2}-5{z}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z & =6{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z-5{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{z}^{4}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{z}^{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{z}^{5}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1} \\\\  & =2{z}^{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}-{z}^{5}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{3}-(-2)^{3})-({1}^{5}-(-2)^{5}) \\\\  & =2(1+8)-(1+32) \\\\  & =18-33 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249731 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250211 1069 "q-114194" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250211 1071 "${\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{8}-3)\\,\\text{d}t=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250211 1072 #uuid "a1ed5e9b-b598-59a6-8472-fc44fb8fbbd6" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250211 1074 "Splitting up the difference and factoring out the constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{8}-3)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{8}\\,\\text{d}t-3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{9}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}-3t\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}({1}^{9}-(-1)^{9})-3(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(1+1)-3(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}-6 \\\\  & =-\\frac{52}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249776 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250212 1069 "q-114202" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250212 1071 "${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(2-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250212 1072 #uuid "646baf8c-2fc5-5cdc-90e1-2b00b50de1be" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250212 1074 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(2-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(2-3{y}^{-2})\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nThen, we split the integral into the difference of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(2-3{y}^{-2})\\text{d}y & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{y}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2y\\,{|}_{-2}^{-1}-3(-{y}^{-1}){|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =2y\\,{|}_{-2}^{-1}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =2(-1-(-2))+3(\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{1}{(-2)}) \\\\  & =2(-1+2)+3(-1+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2(1)+3(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249674 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250213 1069 "q-114239" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250213 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{-x}}=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250213 1072 #uuid "549392a2-2e35-5447-97fd-7403d9669a01" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250213 1074 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{-x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}]^{′}}{{[{e}^{-x}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{2x}{-{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{-x}} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[2x]^{′}}{[{e}^{-x}]^{′}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{2}{-{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250072 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250214 1069 "q-114245" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250214 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250214 1072 #uuid "d22fa8db-1712-5866-9e2b-91e93589e8f9" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250214 1074 "If we plug in $x=1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{{[{x}^{2}-2x+1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{{[2x-2]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249711 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250215 1069 "q-114249" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250215 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250215 1072 #uuid "1490f312-92c6-56e8-8f14-6e1263bd40da" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250215 1074 "If we plug in $x=-1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{[(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1]}^{′}}{{[{x}^{2}+2x+1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{2x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)]}^{′}}{{[2x+2]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x+2})}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{1}{2(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249758 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250216 1069 "q-114250" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250216 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250216 1072 #uuid "20501bc0-b3ab-55d0-9d3e-6b7172e6e273" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250216 1074 "If we plug in $x=1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-2x+1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{2x-2} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & =-{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{x-1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)]^{′}}{[x-1]^{′}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{1} \\\\  & =-{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249820 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250217 1069 "q-114251" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250217 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+1}{({x}^{2}-4)^{2}-12x-21}=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250217 1072 #uuid "bc8be3aa-b527-5785-8803-82defead906e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250217 1074 "If we plug in $x=-1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+1}{({x}^{2}-4)^{2}-12x-21} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}+2x+1]}^{′}}{{[({x}^{2}-4)^{2}-12x-21]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+2}{4x({x}^{2}-4)-12} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x+1}{2x({x}^{2}-4)-6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+1}{({x}^{2}-4)^{2}-12x-21} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x+1}{2x({x}^{2}-4)-6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{[x+1]}^{′}}{{[2x({x}^{2}-4)-6]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{1}{6{x}^{2}-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6(-1)^{2}-8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249922 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250218 1069 "q-114252" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250218 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250218 1072 #uuid "9948b488-cf7d-5e1c-b375-ca22aa59712f" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250218 1074 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}]^{′}}{[{sin}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(x-2){e}^{-x}+4}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0-2){e}^{0}+4}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+4}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250050 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250219 1069 "q-114259" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250219 1071 "![](images/a7/a7330541d6102141aec87f6733d09585553de672c5e55d2f84fe70196e4c6b5f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x),$ where the function $f(x)$ is defined on $[-2,2].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250219 1072 #uuid "fad05a22-89fe-5f89-8760-fc0c9c702ff1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250219 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(-1,1).$ Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval  $(-1,1)$ only." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249685 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250220 1069 "q-114260" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250220 1071 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}$ is concave up." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250220 1072 #uuid "ff7ca54e-f0a7-54c4-924a-f61e267514f6" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250220 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n6x-6>0\\,⟹\\,x>1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249764 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250221 1069 "q-114264" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250221 1071 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)={e}^{1-x}+2x$ is concave down." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250221 1072 #uuid "492ca0b9-b703-572c-bebc-7f98d19e71c1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250221 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)={e}^{1-x}+2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{1-x}+2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-(-{e}^{1-x})={e}^{1-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ Notice that ${f}^{″}(x)={e}^{1-x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave down nowhere." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249825 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250222 1069 "q-114266" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250222 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & 4x+6\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+0x+8\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,8{x}^{2}+0x+8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\boxed{}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,12x+8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{12x+18}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250222 1072 #uuid "25703f58-157a-5c2f-8209-08696d53f155" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250222 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x$-term. So, we write it using a zero coefficient, as follows:\n\n$$\n4{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+0x+8\n$$\n\nUsing the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}-2x+3 \\\\  & 4x+6\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+0x+8\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,8{x}^{2}+0x+8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,8{x}^{2}-12x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,12x+8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{12x+18}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the missing expressions are\n\n$\\,$ ${x}^{2}-2x+3\\,$ and $\\,-8{x}^{2}-12x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249741 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250223 1069 "q-114270" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250223 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250223 1072 #uuid "aab077f8-99df-5fb1-8e2c-0476334114c1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250223 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](images/09/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249977 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250224 1069 "q-114271" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250224 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-5{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty )$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250224 1072 #uuid "3a2a1a5b-82e0-576b-9a2b-9cca3c9ca19e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250224 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}-60{x}^{2} \\\\  & =60{x}^{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{2}(x-1) & >0 \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval   $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning, this leads to the inequality $x-1<0$ with $x\\ne 0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249715 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250225 1069 "q-114272" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250225 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250225 1072 #uuid "136bbef5-7f5b-5272-816c-ea16bb2ceed2" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250225 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}+(2x-4){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x}+(2x-2){e}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x} & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for all real values $x\\ne 0,$ the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ So $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{2}{e}^{x}<0,$ which does not have solutions. So $f(x)$ is concave down nowhere.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249965 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250226 1069 "q-114273" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250226 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,e)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,e)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(e,\\infty )$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250226 1072 #uuid "9e263520-c83b-578e-886f-bcaf93059761" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250226 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =2x(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2x \\\\  & =2x(4-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(4-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2x\\cdot \\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =8-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-4 \\\\  & =4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & <1 \\\\ x & <e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on  $(0,e).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)<0,$ which has the solution $(e,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250090 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250227 1069 "q-114277" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250227 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250227 1072 #uuid "c1339af8-7ec4-512f-92c7-059b9ab8ffee" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250227 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1 & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${x}^{2}>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{3}-1<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249903 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250228 1069 "q-114280" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250228 1071 "![](images/4b/4bb767047996aad50a7991915c91774e0c19d9e5c1a7c60d66dd7a6afef0fa03.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250228 1072 #uuid "572d008c-de8c-52c8-a2da-c20948cfcb3b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250228 1074 "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=-2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=-1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$   the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249716 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250229 1069 "q-114287" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250229 1071 "Calculate $\\int 6x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250229 1072 #uuid "a75c4b14-7006-556e-bd1e-e4613f35b446" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250229 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 6x{e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe, substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\cdot (-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3})\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-3x}+2\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2x{e}^{-3x}+2\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249719 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250230 1069 "q-114288" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250230 1071 "$\\int 4x{e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250230 1072 #uuid "03919aa8-efb9-58ab-8664-d380cf005c1c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250230 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 4x{e}^{8x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=4 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{8x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 4x\\cdot {e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{e}^{8x}-\\int \\frac{1}{8}{e}^{8x}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x{e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{8x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{8x}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{8x}}{16}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250092 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250231 1069 "q-114289" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250231 1071 "Calculate $\\int x{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250231 1072 #uuid "872e80da-db87-5cbe-89ef-83c58a2b3950" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250231 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x{3}^{x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′} & ={3}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\int \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\int \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250105 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250232 1069 "q-114290" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250232 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int (x-2){e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250232 1072 #uuid "623d728c-8114-50ed-8705-4853f0ddb4f0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250232 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int (x-2){e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x-2\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{x+3}\\, & ⟹\\,v={e}^{x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (x-2)\\cdot {e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =(x-2)\\cdot {e}^{x+3}-\\int {e}^{x+3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x-2){e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =(x-2){e}^{x+3}-\\int {e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-2){e}^{x+3}-{e}^{x+3}+C \\\\  & =(x-3){e}^{x+3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250103 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250233 1069 "q-114293" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250233 1071 "Calculate $\\int (3x-4){e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250233 1072 #uuid "1022e01f-7a5a-562e-a877-5ba5b3c9edd4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250233 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int (3x-4){e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x-4\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (3x-4)\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(3x-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3})\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3x-4){e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{(3x-4){e}^{-3x}}{3}+\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3x-4){e}^{-3x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3x-3){e}^{-3x}}{3}+C \\\\  & =(1-x){e}^{-3x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250088 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250234 1069 "q-114294" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250234 1071 "$\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250234 1072 #uuid "de415510-24b2-53a0-b8c3-ddc9cdee62b2" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250234 1074 "We use the identities $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{3}⁡x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249970 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250235 1069 "q-114297" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250235 1071 "$\\int 3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250235 1072 #uuid "a8a77892-fbcb-5fb9-802a-ce425028c881" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250235 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249875 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250236 1069 "q-114299" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250236 1071 "Calculate $\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250236 1072 #uuid "3b188b68-b90e-5d2a-a750-30bfecf83280" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250236 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\int \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250045 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250237 1069 "q-114304" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250237 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250237 1072 #uuid "40079a0f-e072-5ffd-a039-30e177ed76fb" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250237 1074 "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249677 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250238 1069 "q-114306" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250238 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250238 1072 #uuid "edc6fd62-64ca-5ae7-b219-55728bf85a2e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250238 1074 "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3[\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡0] \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249864 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250239 1069 "q-114309" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250239 1071 "$\\int (x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250239 1072 #uuid "64685827-c31f-5450-80d9-3d65e9e828de" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250239 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int (x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+4\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (x+4)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(x+4)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249834 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250240 1069 "q-114311" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250240 1071 "$\\int (2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250240 1072 #uuid "a36b9441-9ddb-586c-961c-0956d2121b3c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250240 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int (2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+3\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (2x+3)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(2x+3)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =-(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249787 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250241 1069 "q-114312" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250241 1071 "$\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250241 1072 #uuid "6d0e94f6-1e93-555f-a728-ca5181d50236" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250241 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{25}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249836 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250242 1069 "q-114313" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250242 1071 "$\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250242 1072 #uuid "e6eaf6d0-3cca-5bcd-87e4-c38716de9570" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250242 1074 "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250006 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250243 1069 "q-114314" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250243 1071 "Calculate $\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250243 1072 #uuid "ea7a186f-be1a-52b9-b47f-3320d955e3e8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250243 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡x\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={csc}^{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ where $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249812 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250244 1069 "q-114318" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250244 1071 "${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250244 1072 #uuid "062977fc-037b-5a67-9a07-c0d038a63f3e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250244 1074 "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-[\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =-[1-\\sqrt{2}] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249892 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250245 1069 "q-114320" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250245 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-1}^{0}3x{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250245 1072 #uuid "ecbdeecb-1f0c-572b-8510-8e18414c1281" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250245 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-2x} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot (-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2})\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x{e}^{-2x}}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}3x{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{3x{e}^{-2x}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{0}+\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x{e}^{-2x}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{0}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-2x}{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[0{e}^{-2(0)}-(-1){e}^{-2(-1)}]-\\frac{3}{4}[{e}^{-2(0)}-{e}^{-2(-1)}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[0+{e}^{2}]-\\frac{3}{4}[1-{e}^{2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}({e}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249915 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250246 1069 "q-114322" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250246 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250246 1072 #uuid "b71aa220-d93a-5e59-b207-8dade0373ae4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250246 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{4}|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{4}|}_{0}^{\\pi }+{\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[-\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )}{4}+\\frac{0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0)}{4}] \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{16}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0)+\\frac{1}{16}(0-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249889 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250247 1069 "q-114323" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250247 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(4,3)$ and the line $x=2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250247 1072 #uuid "a7327f68-1eda-5ad2-867d-ca3941f336b9" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250247 1074 "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/ea/ea64c0752738b0c917d897060351cdd43ed7bbf687cc20781b7241ee3be0ad3c.png)\n\nSince the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(4,3)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between the $x$-coordinate of the point and $2.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|4-2|=2.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249824 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250248 1069 "q-114328" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250248 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250248 1072 #uuid "37affd24-3b4f-536c-8927-723157249685" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250248 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{9}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0)+\\frac{1}{9}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250034 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250249 1069 "q-114329" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250249 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/3}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250249 1072 #uuid "e406eb1a-a97b-5da3-8a7a-44bb2a8c8ae5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250249 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=9x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=9 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 9x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =9x\\cdot (-\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3})\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3x{e}^{-3x}+3\\int {e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3x{e}^{-3x}{|}_{0}^{1/3}+3{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}{e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3x{e}^{-3x}{|}_{0}^{1/3}-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =-3[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3(1/3)}-0\\cdot {e}^{-3\\cdot 0}]-[{e}^{-3(1/3)}-{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =-3[\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-0]-[{e}^{-1}-1] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}-\\frac{1}{e}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-2}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250075 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250250 1069 "q-114330" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250250 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250250 1072 #uuid "0ba270d9-6da5-5830-bec9-26eadbb91904" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250250 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 5x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5})-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{5}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =(0-0)+\\frac{1}{5}(-1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249697 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250251 1069 "q-114336" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250251 1071 "![](images/69/69e06e1b9b72e3a4c1fc2e7f2fcd85aa4aa8b34e4fa2f4f5aebb8d1b10474415.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250251 1072 #uuid "1e7059ea-e4f5-5468-9ed3-af001f239b6b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250251 1074 "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(-3,4).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2+4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =6-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{18}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4)$ is $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249819 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250252 1069 "q-114341" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250252 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1,-5)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250252 1072 #uuid "447c75d4-22ec-5570-87c0-7a6b7bbeb6a9" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250252 1074 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.  \n\n![](images/3a/3a4bbfd80480fe3fcc778a490514d27d65d83abf77ca1752e4203d9b3185b618.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(1,-5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+5 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{17}{3}-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{26}{3} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{26}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3.$ So, our solution is $x=4$ and $y=-3.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(4,-3).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(1,-5)$ and $(4,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4-1)^{2}+(-3+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(1,-5)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249831 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250253 1069 "q-114343" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250253 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250253 1072 #uuid "633d0fd6-e7a8-521b-a27a-54658471a6ed" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250253 1074 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/02/021b08ba0d14e40939103d6d3e9e2004d49e5d047a5f89677b3fe542aa866f82.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x-2$ and passes through the point $(-1,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-(x+1) \\\\ y & =-x-1+5 \\\\ y & =-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x-2 \\\\ y=-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ x+x & =4+2 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=1.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=1.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,5)$ and $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3+1)^{2}+(1-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2$ is $4\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249966 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250254 1069 "q-114345" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250254 1071 "![](images/ff/ff9810290868f7fd465bc310c969255b92f646d6b59ad71a93a3dcc9c38ef49c.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250254 1072 #uuid "dd8e8e07-0b88-58f7-806a-13412093afb7" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250254 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+2x & =3 \\\\ 3y & =-2x+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1$ and passes through the point $(3,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5)$ is  $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249756 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250255 1069 "q-114346" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250255 1071 "![](images/df/df3838ae44d1477a4a0de303972cd26a2ceaad9b213f1d8e4be3a53bd453aeb2.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250255 1072 #uuid "3954acb1-fda8-5e85-a442-60c3272bf340" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250255 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3x & =2 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=3x+2$ and passes through the point $(4,0).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $3,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=3x+2 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{4}{3}-2 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 10x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0)$ is  $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250019 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250256 1069 "q-114349" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250256 1071 "![](images/ba/ba46b4f3f458a6be13ea01ca1351658c102732db94e22725890afaaade6fc904.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $5y-10x=5$ that is closest to the point $(-2,3).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250256 1072 #uuid "855df716-3231-50d2-97cf-6767626cecd3" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250256 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-10x & =5 \\\\ 5y & =10x+5 \\\\ y & =2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=2x+1$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $2,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x+1 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{1}{2}x & =2-1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{9}{5}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{9}{5}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $5y-10x=5$ that is closest to the point $(-2,3)$ is  $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5}).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250064 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250257 1069 "q-114350" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250257 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250257 1072 #uuid "2171a240-7846-596a-ba37-d0a6bdd37eb7" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250257 1074 "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡x=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({csc}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249803 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250258 1069 "q-114353" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250258 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250258 1072 #uuid "5c6112a4-1f7e-5aa3-8161-0a98a140a5f9" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250258 1074 "We use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249713 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250259 1069 "q-114358" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250259 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-5,3)$ and the line $2y+5x=10.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250259 1072 #uuid "6d7dd6e7-50c5-518a-95a2-582c9f1e9bcc" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250259 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+5x & =10 \\\\ 2y & =-5x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.  \n\n![](images/8c/8c4238a00b775d4db828651ae2691596bfadafcdc748494cc957bad1cb1b6b91.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+5$ and passes through the point $(-5,3).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+5) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{5}x+2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+5 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{5}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}x+5 & =\\frac{2}{5}x+5 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{2}{5}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{29}{10}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=5.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(0,5).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-5,3)$ and $(0,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(0-(-5))}^{2}+(5-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-5,3)$ and the line $2y+5x=10$ is $\\sqrt{29}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249996 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250260 1069 "q-114364" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250260 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and the line $2y+3x=-6.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250260 1072 #uuid "9130ee0d-24eb-5b04-a0dc-d2670f4f9892" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250260 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+3x & =-6 \\\\ 2y & =-3x-6 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/71/715e1292edaf78979f6b9ae36586a8196dcc30e865915e7eefba2360281f85ec.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-3$ and passes through the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{5}{6}+3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,-\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,-\\frac{3}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1-2)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and the line $2y+3x=-6$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249866 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250261 1069 "q-114404" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250261 1071 "![](images/0c/0c9e74029cf6761469c72dc76dc6b13ba92107857c2a2d63ca6983dec282db37.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250261 1072 #uuid "801c713f-e967-5d36-84a4-2ee752acb35d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250261 1074 "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection. The function does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\n- The point $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$  the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is not a point of inflection. The function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, there are no points of inflection." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249712 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250262 1069 "q-114405" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250262 1071 "![](images/8b/8b81ef12b7193ecbaf0b9b3c12b5a7c99fd72b4562c4197700062bf654822a8c.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250262 1072 #uuid "350102d7-fd8b-5da5-b1f0-f772776a425a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250262 1074 "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=-1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=-1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=0.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at  $x=1,$  the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=-1$ only." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249991 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250263 1069 "q-114409" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250263 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250263 1072 #uuid "2b32253a-ede8-5f55-9a10-7398b09c31f0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250263 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-2x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (1-x)(1+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1$  and $x=1.$ So, we pick points around them, say $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =\\frac{2(1-(-2)^{2})}{(1+(-2)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{25}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{2(1-(0)^{2})}{(1+(0)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =2>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{2(1-(2)^{2})}{(1+(2)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1$ and $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are indeed points of inflection." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249728 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250264 1069 "q-11441" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250264 1071 "Calculate $A-B$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250264 1072 #uuid "8b6c3668-a477-577e-a262-5b7cb2e83845" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250264 1074 "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5-3 & 5-2 \\\\ 5-1 & 4-0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250004 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250265 1069 "q-114413" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250265 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-4x+7).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250265 1072 #uuid "36051cdc-8f6e-5b15-aa00-2922341825f4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250265 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-4x+7)+{e}^{x}(2x-4) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3)+{e}^{x}(2x-2) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}({x}^{2}+1)>0$ for all real values of $x,$ the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249946 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250266 1069 "q-114414" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250266 1071 "For the function $f(x)=x-{e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250266 1072 #uuid "a75ae1b4-9111-5369-af8d-34d27de2f589" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250266 1074 "We need to identify whether $x=0$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=x-{e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(0)=-1<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(0)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=0\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250115 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250267 1069 "q-114415" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250267 1071 "For the function $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250267 1072 #uuid "b90d338d-3db1-5cee-ac01-ae8e8b265add" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250267 1074 "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1+2x){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({x}^{2}+x)\\cdot {e}^{x}+(2x+1){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+3x+1){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so there is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is false. $x=1$ is not a critical point.\n\n- Statement IV is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249835 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250268 1069 "q-114416" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250268 1071 "![](images/82/826336bca267300c9b98e43072f74bb5efdacff0a4007fb7b938eb61893d8fbe.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,4).$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250268 1072 #uuid "a5f8d68d-fe8f-5336-aee6-29d6bc4b8f68" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250268 1074 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has point of inflection at $x=2$ only." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250014 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250269 1069 "q-114419" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250269 1071 "![](images/03/036e249b188b46fe94bd6955fce114cb11dc0f1e1c169fb58d36135cfbc9b797.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250269 1072 #uuid "f1aa692a-6d96-551a-a256-b72aca703023" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250269 1074 "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has no inflection points." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250080 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250270 1069 "q-114423" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250270 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250270 1072 #uuid "78ecd097-3d73-59be-9c42-4cecf987949e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250270 1074 "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250066 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250271 1069 "q-114429" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250271 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-{sec}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250271 1072 #uuid "590fb4d1-46ef-5eb8-ba9c-0c9e6a8189ba" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250271 1074 "First, we select the identity that contains a $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-{sec}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-[\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =-[2-\\sqrt{2}] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249829 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250272 1069 "q-114430" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250272 1071 "$\\int (1+2x+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250272 1072 #uuid "e264cd7f-f120-56f7-87e8-c3568e011399" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250272 1074 "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1+2x+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+{tan}^{2}⁡x+2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x+2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249755 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250273 1069 "q-114431" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250273 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250273 1072 #uuid "26865ace-62b5-521c-a5d8-413469434ebb" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250273 1074 "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{({tan}^{2}⁡x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249896 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250274 1069 "q-114435" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250274 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{3}⁡x+{tan}^{2}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250274 1072 #uuid "47d60902-14e4-57db-9a3c-83c4f5318199" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250274 1074 "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{3}⁡x+{tan}^{2}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡x(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249976 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250275 1069 "q-114441" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250275 1071 "${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cot}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250275 1072 #uuid "3841fdbd-8341-5e24-9f59-0d6b99ab3106" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250275 1074 "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.$ Rearranging this gives\n\n$$\n{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{cot}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}({csc}^{2}⁡x-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-x){|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{\\pi }{4}] \\\\  & =[-0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[-1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249732 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250276 1069 "q-114454" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250276 1071 "${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2+3{x}^{2}+2{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250276 1072 #uuid "5ae308dc-8f77-58de-a2c1-ae20d02ecf13" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250276 1074 "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2+3{x}^{2}+2{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)+3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2{csc}^{2}⁡x+3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{x}^{3}){|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}-[-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}] \\\\  & =-0+\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{8}+2(1)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{64} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{7{\\pi }^{3}}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249786 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250277 1069 "q-114457" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250277 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{cot}^{5}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250277 1072 #uuid "838376fb-3080-54c1-8fed-02688e1a872e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250277 1074 "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{cot}^{5}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x(1-{cot}^{4}⁡x)}{(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}{(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int -\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250082 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250278 1069 "q-114460" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250278 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}({sin}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x)\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250278 1072 #uuid "8dcffc55-70b6-5a56-9948-97e923b1fff7" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250278 1074 "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}({sin}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{3}⁡x(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{3}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{3}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249986 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250279 1069 "q-114467" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250279 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡(3x+2)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x+2){sin}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250279 1072 #uuid "35e08417-e875-5f7f-a591-7aa6eac3199b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250279 1074 "From the identity ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ with $\\theta =3x+2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(3x+2)+{sin}^{2}⁡(3x+2)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡(3x+2)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x+2){sin}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡(3x+2)({cos}^{2}⁡(3x+2)+{sin}^{2}⁡(3x+2))}\\,\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+2)+C & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250063 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250280 1069 "q-114469" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250280 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250280 1072 #uuid "a520c33d-1aa5-5864-8fa9-daf66894b11e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250280 1074 "From the identity $1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ with $\\theta =2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{tan}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})={sec}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}\\,\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{[{tan}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+1]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}\\,\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}\\,\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\int \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})}\\,\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{5})+C & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249885 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250281 1069 "q-114473" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250281 1071 "$\\int (8+8{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250281 1072 #uuid "7da788f3-581f-57f3-a61d-5c56509defca" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250281 1074 "From the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ with $\\theta =4x+3,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3)={csc}^{2}⁡(4x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (8+8{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x & =8\\int (1+{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int {csc}^{2}⁡(4x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8[-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)]+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250085 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250282 1069 "q-114492" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250282 1071 "What is the remainder when  ${x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-10x-7$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+2x+1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250282 1072 #uuid "3bde49c7-b448-53e6-a9c0-5dedb4a26185" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250282 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}-4x+3 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+2x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-10x-7\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-10x-7 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}-4x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{2}-6x-7 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{3{x}^{2}+6x+3}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,12x-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is ${x}^{2}-4x+3,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-12x-10.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249897 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250283 1069 "q-114510" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250283 1071 "Determine $\\int \\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡(5x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250283 1072 #uuid "8ce9a833-7138-5c70-a343-cfc3b184c5d8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250283 1074 "From the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ with $\\theta =5x-2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡(5x-2)={csc}^{2}⁡(5x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡(5x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{[{cot}^{2}⁡(5x-2)+1]\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{csc}^{2}⁡(5x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\int \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(5x-2)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & = \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-2)\\text{d}x & = \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-2)+C & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249680 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250284 1069 "q-114512" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250284 1071 "What is the quotient of $2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}-1$ divided by $2{x}^{2}+1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250284 1072 #uuid "c3376910-e30f-5e67-8b1e-add6d105eb70" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250284 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the ${x}^{3}$ and $x$ terms. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing terms with zero coefficients, as follows:\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}-1=2{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+0x-1\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}-1 \\\\  & 2{x}^{2}+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+0x-1\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,2{x}^{2}+0x-1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+0x-1}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is ${x}^{2}-1,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249956 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250285 1069 "q-114515" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250285 1071 "What is the quotient of ${x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}-4$ divided by ${x}^{2}+1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250285 1072 #uuid "90f955b5-c727-5e31-93df-e2d17bebf606" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250285 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the ${x}^{3}$ and $x$ terms. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing terms with zero coefficients, as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}-4={x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+0x-4\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}-4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+0x-4\\,\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-4{x}^{2}+0x-4 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{2}+0x-4}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is ${x}^{2}-4,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249816 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250286 1069 "q-114522" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250286 1071 "What is the remainder when ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-1$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250286 1072 #uuid "4cfcf83f-ab76-50ea-b191-56bfee4c4ea6" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250286 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the ${x}^{2}$ and $x$ terms. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing terms with a coefficient of zero, as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-1={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+0x-1\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+2x-1 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+0x-1\\,\\,\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+0x-1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+2x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}-2x-1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-{x}^{2}+0x-1}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is ${x}^{2}+2x-1,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-2x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249778 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250287 1069 "q-114551" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250287 1071 "Calculate $\\int 9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250287 1072 #uuid "3faaa55a-7a42-5cea-8d42-e9f7ac430290" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250287 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=9{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 9{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=3{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)(3{x}^{3})-\\int \\frac{3{x}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{x}^{3}+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249960 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250288 1069 "q-114552" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250288 1071 "Calculate $\\int 2x{log}_{5}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250288 1072 #uuid "91292256-4f82-5669-95d9-e91e8b8d6af8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250288 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{5}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ {v}^{′}=2x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x{log}_{5}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(x)({x}^{2})-\\int ({x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{5}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249742 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250289 1069 "q-114553" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250289 1071 "Calculate $\\int x{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250289 1072 #uuid "0ce30fbb-1b42-559b-a553-f2eb05e4f3a4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250289 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249761 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250290 1069 "q-114555" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250290 1071 "Calculate $\\int 8{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250290 1072 #uuid "9fadab91-be05-5653-9838-592471a9067d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250290 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=8{x}^{3}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 8{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=2{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\cdot (2{x}^{4})-\\int \\frac{2{x}^{4}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+C \\\\  & ={x}^{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249898 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250291 1069 "q-114556" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250291 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{log}_{4}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250291 1072 #uuid "67ee7e99-c327-5643-bad9-04c09862c4b7" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250291 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{4}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{log}_{4}⁡(2x)}{3{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{log}_{4}⁡(2x)}{3{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-\\frac{1}{9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1}{9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{4}⁡(2x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249718 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250292 1069 "q-114557" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250292 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250292 1072 #uuid "362beb84-a4c5-596a-b73b-e0ca0f79f0fe" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250292 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})-\\int (-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{4}}+2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{4}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{2{x}^{4}}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249953 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250293 1069 "q-114579" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250293 1071 "$\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250293 1072 #uuid "ac6118b3-9859-5ff7-afb9-8425a21afcf2" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250293 1074 "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\,{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot x-\\int x\\cdot \\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249745 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250294 1069 "q-114585" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250294 1071 "$\\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250294 1072 #uuid "805d5bf3-56e5-56a7-8a77-c98eb5fe1df0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250294 1074 "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{2}{2x+1} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int u\\,{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\int \\frac{2x}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use a substitution $2x+1=u,$ thus $2x=u-1$ and $\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{3}⁡(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\int (\\frac{2x}{2x+1})\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\int \\frac{u-1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\,\\text{d}u+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\frac{u}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{2}+D] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\frac{2x+1}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)}{2}+D] \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-2x}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=D-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}$ is an integral constant." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249840 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250295 1069 "q-114592" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250295 1071 "$\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250295 1072 #uuid "eec9484d-20c7-527b-9679-505624b164e9" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250295 1074 "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-3} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\,{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\cdot x-\\int x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-\\int \\frac{x}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use a substitution $x-3=u,$ thus $x=u+3$ and $\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-\\int \\frac{x}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-\\int \\frac{u+3}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-\\int \\,\\text{d}u-3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-u-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡u+D \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-(x-3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)+D \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-x+3+D \\\\  & =(x-3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=D+3$ is an integral constant." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250073 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250296 1069 "q-114597" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250296 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1/3}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250296 1072 #uuid "a5747f9d-1d5b-5a68-8df4-e21d4a5aef56" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250296 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/3}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/3}^{e}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1/3}^{e}-{\\int }_{1/3}^{e}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){|}_{1/3}^{e}-{\\int }_{1/3}^{e}x(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3e)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-{\\int }_{1/3}^{e}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3e)-x{|}_{1/3}^{e}\\, \\\\  & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3e)-(e-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+e-e+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249791 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250297 1069 "q-114600" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250297 1071 "Calculate $\\int (2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250297 1072 #uuid "228e02d3-780d-535b-b966-624ae49969cb" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250297 1074 "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nRearranging the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249733 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250298 1069 "q-11462" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250298 1071 "Consider the matrix\n    $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nWhat are the dimensions of ${C}^{T}?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250298 1072 #uuid "55ec6bf0-28c7-50e4-93fc-37f45d7409ca" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250298 1074 "When a matrix is transposed, its dimensions switch from $m\\times n$ to $n\\times m.$\n\nSo, since $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the transpose ${C}^{T}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249672 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250299 1069 "q-11467" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250299 1071 "Consider the matrix\n    $C=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}].$\nWhat are the dimensions of ${C}^{T}?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250299 1072 #uuid "70f0f1fb-3f62-5f35-8f53-9297e4389129" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250299 1074 "When a matrix is transposed, its dimensions switch from $m\\times n$ to $n\\times m.$\n\nSo, since $C$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix, the transpose ${C}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250054 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250300 1069 "q-114677" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250300 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250300 1072 #uuid "16bb2470-b172-54fa-bb93-68ffa12f7218" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250300 1074 "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249990 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250301 1069 "q-114679" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250301 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250301 1072 #uuid "91699790-f225-57ae-8ea8-89f90b88a5ce" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250301 1074 "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[uv{|}_{1/2}^{1}-{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x){|}_{1/2}^{1}-{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}x(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 1)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})-{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-x{|}_{1/2}^{1}\\,] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0-(1-\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249850 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250302 1069 "q-11468" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250302 1071 "Find ${F}^{T}$ given that \n    $F=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250302 1072 #uuid "ba9cac91-a5f4-5f2e-ae2a-31b596a9dde6" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250302 1074 "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns. \n\nHere, there is just a single column, so it becomes a single row:\n\n$$\n{F}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249673 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250303 1069 "q-114681" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250303 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250303 1072 #uuid "4ba36d0b-ab92-5237-aa17-794cfb598845" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250303 1074 "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{5x}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x]}_{0}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{10})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot [0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249967 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250304 1069 "q-114685" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250304 1071 "$\\int (3{x}^{2}-{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250304 1072 #uuid "005ce8c5-ca00-5c75-b19b-e74315abd5f2" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250304 1074 "We can use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}-{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249676 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250305 1069 "q-114687" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250305 1071 "Calculate $\\int (4-{cos}^{2}⁡4x+{sin}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250305 1072 #uuid "a74426e1-c72a-5f3f-acc7-d171cc86f36f" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250305 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =4x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x={cos}^{2}⁡4x-{sin}^{2}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4-{cos}^{2}⁡4x+{sin}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (4-[{cos}^{2}⁡4x-{sin}^{2}⁡4x])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (4-\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250067 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250306 1069 "q-114688" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250306 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({sin}^{2}⁡4x-{cos}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250306 1072 #uuid "6789bdc9-30dd-5df6-91b5-90b18efe1bb7" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250306 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =4x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x={cos}^{2}⁡4x-{sin}^{2}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({sin}^{2}⁡4x-{cos}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({cos}^{2}⁡4x-{sin}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249973 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250307 1069 "q-114691" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250307 1071 "$\\int 4{cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250307 1072 #uuid "87059ed3-91f0-5463-8df6-00c144965662" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250307 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249899 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250308 1069 "q-114693" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250308 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250308 1072 #uuid "1074dd87-580f-51cd-9b83-51cf6a648213" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250308 1074 "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+1)(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2})(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-4\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}(2x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-4(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}(2x-1)+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}{2}-{e}^{2x}(2x-1)-4D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}(4{x}^{2}-4x+3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-4D.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249686 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250309 1069 "q-114695" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250309 1071 "Calculate $\\int ({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250309 1072 #uuid "c11ce04f-84d3-5cfd-9c93-5cd848d912ec" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250309 1074 "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}-4\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4)({e}^{x})-\\int ({e}^{x})(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}-2\\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x({e}^{x})-\\int ({e}^{x})(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}-\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}-{e}^{x}+D \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}-2({e}^{x}(x-1)+D) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4){e}^{x}-2(x-1){e}^{x}-2D \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x-2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249759 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250310 1069 "q-114696" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250310 1071 "Calculate $\\int 4{cos}^{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250310 1072 #uuid "c6cefcd4-0ca3-54cc-a93f-201d1a8af19f" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250310 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\theta =x$ and substituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{cos}^{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {(2{cos}^{2}⁡x)}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the same formula $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1$ with $\\theta =2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\frac{3}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+\\frac{3}{2}\\,x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{3x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249972 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250311 1069 "q-114698" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250311 1071 "$\\int {cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250311 1072 #uuid "4cfdf230-d427-5945-b883-cca5a7c6a112" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250311 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249802 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250312 1069 "q-114699" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250312 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int (1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250312 1072 #uuid "c03c84f2-a54b-5cd7-81f9-a3bec98efab8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250312 1074 "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=1-2{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(1-2{x}^{2})(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4})(-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}}{4}+\\int x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4})(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(4x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}}{4}+(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(4x-1)+C) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}}{4}+\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(4x-1)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{16}(-8{x}^{2}+4x+3)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249702 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250313 1069 "q-114703" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250313 1071 "$\\int ({sin}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250313 1072 #uuid "7bceb2aa-4302-5440-9a58-9fffc3e95c17" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250313 1074 "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)={sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({sin}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249944 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250314 1069 "q-114706" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250314 1071 "$\\int (4{sin}^{2}⁡x+6x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250314 1072 #uuid "18d4b96f-8021-5acc-99b3-397a7e245847" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250314 1074 "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{sin}^{2}⁡x+6x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (2\\cdot 2{sin}^{2}⁡x+6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x-2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+6(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\  & =2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+3{x}^{2}+C \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249760 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250315 1069 "q-114709" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250315 1071 "$\\int 2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250315 1072 #uuid "638cdd8d-a193-51d5-8d07-1c091b8aea29" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250315 1074 "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int [1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250041 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250316 1069 "q-114712" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250316 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250316 1072 #uuid "f620a8f2-ded3-5ab5-a180-a9e0b77ecbff" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250316 1074 "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249810 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250317 1069 "q-114879" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250317 1071 "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250317 1072 #uuid "6f255ed2-421f-593a-aea8-7435217040f2" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250317 1074 "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & 3 & 1 \\\\  & 3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+1}{x-1}=3x+1+\\frac{2}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x+1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3x+1+\\frac{2}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250111 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250318 1069 "q-11488" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250318 1071 "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $y=4{x}^{2}-1$ and $y=3{x}^{2}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250318 1072 #uuid "420c0e34-183e-5e05-8101-22873fd779c2" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250318 1074 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](images/4e/4e3758ddf652c3dd7df10d381b42ca1dde50d48250bed672b740329191202597.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/54/54ad3b4c8bc3aef707af764cd8d8badf9390737dc75491aa66ea554f531a7576.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4{x}^{2}-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=3{x}^{2}.$\n\nWe can now set up the integration with our $x$ values (Top-Bottom):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(3{x}^{2})-(4{x}^{2}-1)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the integrand is an even function. Therefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2((1-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3})-0) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250071 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250319 1069 "q-114880" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250319 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250319 1072 #uuid "40de2008-379c-588d-bf38-59107f150b43" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250319 1074 "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{4}⁡(x))(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(x{log}_{4}⁡(x)-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D) \\\\  & =x{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2x{log}_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =x({({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡4})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250057 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250320 1069 "q-114883" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250320 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250320 1072 #uuid "25b1fd19-3728-5b2c-98ff-e3d6d85297ff" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250320 1074 "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}))(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}))(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3x+D) \\\\  & =x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})+18x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-6D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})+18x]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[e{ln}^{2}⁡({e}^{3})-6e\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})+18e]-[(1){ln}^{2}⁡({1}^{3})-6(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{3})+18(1)] \\\\  & =9e-18e+18e-18 \\\\  & =9e-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249962 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250321 1069 "q-114892" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250321 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3x-4}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250321 1072 #uuid "759dad06-62ad-59d4-9d65-70a46d8005a9" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250321 1074 "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3x-4}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3x-4}{2(x+\\frac{3}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3x-4}{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 3 & -4 \\\\  & ↓ & -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & 3 & -\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-4}{(x+\\frac{3}{2})} & =3-\\frac{(\\frac{17}{2})}{(x+\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{17}{2(x+\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{17}{2x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3x-4}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3-\\frac{17}{2x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[3{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x-17{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[3x-\\frac{17}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[3(2-0)-\\frac{17}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2(2)+3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2(0)+3|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[6-\\frac{17}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[6-\\frac{17}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})] \\\\  & =3-\\frac{17}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250070 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250322 1069 "q-114894" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250322 1071 "${\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2x+5}{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250322 1072 #uuid "cf68c363-607b-5ed8-bb76-a69145831fba" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250322 1074 "First, we pull out the factor of $3$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2x+5}{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2x+5}{3(x-\\frac{1}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2x+5}{(x-\\frac{1}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 2 & 5 \\\\  & ↓ & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & 2 & \\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+5}{(x-\\frac{1}{3})} & =2+\\frac{(\\frac{17}{3})}{(x-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{17}{3(x-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{17}{3x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2x+5}{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2+\\frac{17}{3x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[2{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\text{d}x+17{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{1}{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[2x+\\frac{17}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-1|]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[2(0-(-1))+\\frac{17}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3(0)-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3(-1)-1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[2(1)+\\frac{17}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[2-\\frac{17}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[2-\\frac{34}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}[3-17\\operatorname{ln}⁡2].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249964 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250323 1069 "q-114896" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250323 1071 "${\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250323 1072 #uuid "462b058c-0216-5b9a-aa39-6bb6d7bc768e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250323 1074 "First, we pull out the factor of $6$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6(x+\\frac{1}{6})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{6})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & 2 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & 2 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{6})} & =2+\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3})}{(x+\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{4}{6x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{2x+1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}(2+\\frac{4}{6x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[2{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-1/12}^{0}\\frac{1}{6x+1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[2x+\\frac{4}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6x+1|]}_{-1/12}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[2x+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6x+1|]}_{-1/12}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[2(0-(-\\frac{1}{12}))+\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6(0)+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6(-\\frac{1}{12})+1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[2(\\frac{1}{12})+\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}(1+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249796 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250324 1069 "q-114898" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250324 1071 "${\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{8x+1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250324 1072 #uuid "e67ca108-b4a2-50be-81c1-fe32c84586b1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250324 1074 "First, we pull out the factor of $4$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{8x+1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{8x+1}{4(x-\\frac{1}{4})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{8x+1}{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 8 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & 2 \\\\  & 8 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x+1}{(x-\\frac{1}{4})} & =8+\\frac{3}{(x-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =8+\\frac{12}{4x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{8x+1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}(8+\\frac{12}{4x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8{\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\text{d}x+12{\\int }_{-1/8}^{0}\\frac{1}{4x-1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-1|]_{-1/8}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8(0-(-\\frac{1}{8}))+3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4(0)-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4(-\\frac{1}{8})-1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8(\\frac{1}{8})+3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[1-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249934 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250325 1069 "q-114900" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250325 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250325 1072 #uuid "dca36867-5a6f-51bd-9e64-ad043dde12a1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250325 1074 "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x=2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{3}⁡x)}^{2}(2{x}^{2})-\\int (2{x}^{2})(\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡x)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$ \n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2-2+1)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249921 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250326 1069 "q-114910" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250326 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+18}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250326 1072 #uuid "4c6784ae-fdbb-5b66-87d7-0cf88bfc85a0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250326 1074 "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+18 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+({3}^{2}-{3}^{2})+18 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2})-9+18 \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+18}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-3)/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x-3}{3}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-3}{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+18}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-3)/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249978 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250327 1069 "q-114918" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250327 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{(6x+1)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250327 1072 #uuid "efd4eb6a-b650-553c-bcc2-ab2b63a8dbe2" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250327 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-(6x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4(1-(6x+1)^{2}/4)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(6x+1)^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-(6x+1)^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(6x+1)^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((6x+1)/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{6x+1}{2}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{6x+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-(6x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((6x+1)/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6x+1}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249906 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250328 1069 "q-114923" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250328 1071 "$\\int \\frac{2}{|x-3|\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250328 1072 #uuid "d48157cd-3ea4-5a4b-b2a2-ea19ff6fa51b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250328 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x-3|\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|x-3|\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-3).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x-3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x-3|\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|x-3|\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-3)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250089 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250329 1069 "q-114926" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250329 1071 "$\\int \\frac{4}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250329 1072 #uuid "d6c81b22-316a-514d-b9b9-16489eee9df0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250329 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(2x-7).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=2x-7.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x-7)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249904 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250330 1069 "q-114928" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250330 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250330 1072 #uuid "07920739-bd19-5b4b-b343-8e19ac7c6d5e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250330 1074 "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+15 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+15 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-8x+16)-16+15 \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{(x-4)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-4.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{(x-4)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(x-4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249880 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250331 1069 "q-114929" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250331 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250331 1072 #uuid "fd2cd352-82c1-595a-88b3-99447daccebc" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250331 1074 "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+3 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+3 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+4x+4)-4+3 \\\\  & =(x+2)^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x+2.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(x+2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249805 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250332 1069 "q-114942" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250332 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250332 1072 #uuid "c7237523-38ec-5167-93d0-30747905ffb7" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250332 1074 "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=9{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 9{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=3{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}(3{x}^{3})-\\int (3{x}^{3})(\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡x)(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D) \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{6D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{3})]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{2}{3})-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3-2+\\frac{2}{3})-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(0-0+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249861 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250333 1069 "q-114944" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250333 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250333 1072 #uuid "ee94ab2f-1f66-5795-8930-e1ab98c8dab8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250333 1074 "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\int (x)(\\frac{4{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int {log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})-\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})-\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =x{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{4x{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{8x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{8x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}({ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+8)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{({log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}({ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+8)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}({ln}^{2}⁡({e}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})+8)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}({ln}^{2}⁡({1}^{2})-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(4-8+8)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{4e-8}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249916 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250334 1069 "q-114946" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250334 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250334 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250334 1072 #uuid "d87d568c-f1e1-5dbc-b0f7-7053ed2197d7" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250334 1074 "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=25{x}^{4} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 25{x}^{4}\\text{d}x=5{x}^{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(5{x}^{5})-\\int (5{x}^{5})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-10\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{4} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{4}\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})(\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {x}^{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{25}+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-10\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-10(\\frac{{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{25}+D) \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}-10D \\\\  & ={x}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{5})+C\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-10D.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}25{x}^{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{5})]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[{e}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{2}{5})]-[{1}^{5}(5{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{2}{5})] \\\\  & =[{e}^{5}(5-2+\\frac{2}{5})]-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{17{e}^{5}-2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250029 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250335 1069 "q-114950" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250335 1071 "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250335 1072 #uuid "2ca1f5ad-5834-5a52-8770-140ed254c545" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250335 1074 "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{e}^{-3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-3{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{e}^{-3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-3{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3J \\\\ I & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3(-{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-9I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249849 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250336 1069 "q-114952" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250336 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250336 1072 #uuid "4f09819e-da12-5415-9b7c-4388f9ed375d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250336 1074 "First, we let\n\n$$\nI=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡x(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4}-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4}-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4}-2I \\\\ 3I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡x}{12}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250037 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250337 1069 "q-114965" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250337 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250337 1072 #uuid "85a3e0f2-59ad-5501-8562-0840b6094216" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250337 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{6}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6{ln}^{5}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{6}⁡x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(\\frac{6{ln}^{5}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{7}⁡x-6\\int \\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{7}⁡x-6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{7}⁡x-6I \\\\ 7I & ={ln}^{7}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{7}⁡x}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{6}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}({ln}^{7}⁡x){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}({ln}^{7}⁡(e)-{ln}^{7}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250062 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250338 1069 "q-114999" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250338 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250338 1072 #uuid "aafbcc52-b05c-5248-b40a-c0ce083a128b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250338 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{9}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{9{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{9}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4})(\\frac{9{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4}-9\\int \\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4}-9I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4}-9I \\\\ 10I & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{10}⁡x}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{40}({ln}^{10}⁡x){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}({ln}^{10}⁡(e)-{ln}^{10}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249999 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250339 1069 "q-115031" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250339 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{2x+1}-1\\le 2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250339 1072 #uuid "9ed9bfbd-adb2-54e5-bf5d-6caa34443565" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250339 1074 "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{2x+1}-1 & \\le 2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2x+1} & \\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{2x+1})}^{3} & \\le {3}^{3} \\\\ 2x+1 & \\le 27 \\\\ 2x & \\le 26 \\\\ x & \\le 13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249959 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250340 1069 "q-115044" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250340 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{7x-8}+5<1.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250340 1072 #uuid "7824d943-a7bc-5e31-b589-ff09eec257d6" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250340 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7x-8}+5 & <1 \\\\ \\sqrt{7x-8} & <-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is less than a negative number $(-4),$ so no number that we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, there is no solution to the given inequality." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249726 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250341 1069 "q-115053" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250341 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{9-2x}-3>-4.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250341 1072 #uuid "9a66324c-2933-53c5-8eb6-b60592d5de2a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250341 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9-2x}-3 & >-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{9-2x} & >-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-1),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2x & \\ge -9 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249914 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250342 1069 "q-115060" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250342 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{x+2}-4>-2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250342 1072 #uuid "6c2efa64-c4bf-59c9-a9ea-6f12963b4810" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250342 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+2}-4 & >-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+2} & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+2})}^{2} & >{2}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & >4 \\\\ x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(2,\\infty ).$ The domain of the square root is $x\\ge -2,$ which can be expressed as $[-2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(2,\\infty )\\cap [-2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (2,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250051 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250343 1069 "q-115063" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250343 1071 "Solve the inequality  ${x}^{2}\\ge 49$ using a graphical method." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250343 1072 #uuid "f6818fdc-30f5-5c14-8973-e7ce85d6370f" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250343 1074 "Bringing all the terms to the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 49 \\\\ {x}^{2}-49 & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-49\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-49 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{7}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-7)(x+7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 7.$ \n\nSolving the inequality ${x}^{2}-49\\ge 0$ is equivalent to finding the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-49$ is above the $x$-axis or intersects the $x$-axis (where $y\\ge 0$).\n\nSince the leading coefficient of the quadratic expression is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution set lies outside the roots (including them), as shown below.\n\n![](images/59/5993bb434fca17beb9842d7963c5f560b4d8ef0bacadea4c120160d2ab93d131.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [7,\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249933 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250344 1069 "q-115071" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250344 1071 "Solve the inequality  ${x}^{2}<81$ using a graphical method." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250344 1072 #uuid "fab51149-b042-5e4c-8ab2-7f22ec2997b5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250344 1074 "Bringing all the terms to the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & <81 \\\\ {x}^{2}-81 & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-81\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-81 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{9}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-9)(x+9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 9.$ \n\nSolving the inequality ${x}^{2}-81<0$ is equivalent to finding the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-81$ is below the $x$-axis (where $y<0$).\n\nSince the leading coefficient of the quadratic expression is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution set lies between the roots (but it does not include them), as shown below.\n\n![](images/cc/cc6a957d86d1bc08dfcb7a8e855bb45b41c11c85516c4fed8a6b8c41c1aee6f2.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-9,9).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250007 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250345 1069 "q-115079" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250345 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}\\ge 36$ using the absolute value method." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250345 1072 #uuid "462e4238-8350-58d6-abbb-bbfced08934b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250345 1074 "Taking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 36 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{36} \\\\ |x| & \\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have\n\n$$\nx\\le -6\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-6]\\cup [6,\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250026 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250346 1069 "q-115100" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250346 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{3x-1}{(x+1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+1}-\\frac{4}{(x+1)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $A?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250346 1072 #uuid "6f33e27a-8544-5a94-a218-8ccd29bdc79a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250346 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & =A(x+1)-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)-1 & =A(0+1)-4 \\\\ -1 & =A-4 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249727 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250347 1069 "q-115101" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250347 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5x-22}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{5}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250347 1072 #uuid "76685609-49ea-56b3-87fb-774aa7271a5f" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250347 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-4)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-22 & =5(x-4)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=4$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(4)-22 & =5(4-4)+B \\\\ -2 & =0+B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249895 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250348 1069 "q-115102" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250348 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+5}+\\frac{B}{(x+5)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250348 1072 #uuid "3b54e2bd-fefa-5afb-a65d-851689e94ad5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250348 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+5)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =1(x+5)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-1$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+1 & =1(-1+5)+B \\\\ 0 & =4+B \\\\ B & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250094 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250349 1069 "q-115103" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250349 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+20}{(x+6)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+6}+\\frac{2}{(x+6)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $A?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250349 1072 #uuid "1a85d07c-1ba9-59a3-bef0-8b53f112c995" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250349 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+6)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+20 & =A(x+6)+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+20 & =A(0+6)+2 \\\\ 20 & =6A+2 \\\\ 18 & =6A \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249993 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250350 1069 "q-115105" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250350 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+8}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{5}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250350 1072 #uuid "bad7827a-8edd-5f48-adad-5ff7a9ed20bf" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250350 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+8 & =5(x+2)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-2)+8 & =5(-2+2)+B \\\\ -2 & =0+B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249821 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250351 1069 "q-115119" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250351 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{-2x-1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants, what is the value of $A\\cdot B?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250351 1072 #uuid "f30fa470-8268-51c9-b5a1-d77b47694f51" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250351 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-1)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-1 & =A(x-1)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(1)-1 & =A(1-1)+B \\\\ -3 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(0)-1 & =A(0-1)-3 \\\\ -1 & =-A-3 \\\\ -A & =2 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B=(-2)\\cdot (-3)=6.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249893 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250352 1069 "q-115123" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250352 1071 "![](images/cf/cf20c8dba07a1c6c2e6e6a95fbe45675301e734c976b8073d67ff4bd2cb21ad6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250352 1072 #uuid "e837072a-c08e-5c7a-88b6-aa68dac3c245" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250352 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=6$ and the vertical radius has length $b=9.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249703 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250353 1069 "q-115124" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250353 1071 "![](images/76/764f25a4b18f3f28110c0ce3345402bf183d61cac350832663e221b0de9852a5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250353 1072 #uuid "522f5175-a019-5a90-95ed-58cc045a0980" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250353 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249734 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250354 1069 "q-115126" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250354 1071 "![](images/62/62b62eb01c13ad4eef0367999445402d5a39710b7cfb2df11813b2bfcddb4b9c.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250354 1072 #uuid "04f04ac8-2bb9-5d12-9232-9b8b1040f8d5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250354 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=8.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{8}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{64} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249936 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250355 1069 "q-115129" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250355 1071 "![](images/c7/c71a52cc148f8ce4a23a2fb2005757c9d141d3caf05586d8fb5122f591dc7474.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250355 1072 #uuid "ba6b3820-d361-5a84-a78c-c8c951e27981" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250355 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=1$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250027 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250356 1069 "q-115132" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250356 1071 "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250356 1072 #uuid "0af912c0-685f-5b27-b24c-c0de6bcff1d8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250356 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=7.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=7,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{7}=2\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249886 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250357 1069 "q-115135" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250357 1071 "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250357 1072 #uuid "41a42db1-4c6e-506f-8c7d-8db6141e0014" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250357 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=100$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=100,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{100}=10.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=10,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 10=20,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250112 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250358 1069 "q-115205" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250358 1071 "Solve the inequality $2{e}^{x-2}+1<3.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250358 1072 #uuid "5c7e7d0e-6a38-575b-b57f-c4b68672e279" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250358 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x-2}+1 & <3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x-2} & <2 \\\\ {e}^{x-2} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-2}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\ x-2 & <0 \\\\ x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249943 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250359 1069 "q-115206" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250359 1071 "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250359 1072 #uuid "2a47dc9e-71f1-5fb5-bcb1-b46747ed60f1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250359 1074 "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250102 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250360 1069 "q-115212" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250360 1071 "Solve the inequality $2{(0.7)}^{x+9}\\ge 4.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250360 1072 #uuid "0f5e0fac-aee0-5b85-84d2-7ffd9e4a6cd9" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250360 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(0.7)}^{x+9} & \\ge 4 \\\\ {(0.7)}^{x+9} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.7)^{x+9}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ (x+9)\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7 & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+9 & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.7}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249873 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250361 1069 "q-115213" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250361 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{4}{(0.62)}^{x+5}\\le 1.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250361 1072 #uuid "b87c84f0-c228-5c21-ba35-375ecbb41ef5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250361 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{(0.62)}^{x+5}\\le 1 \\\\ {(0.62)}^{x+5}\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.62)^{x+5}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ (x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62 & \\le 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & \\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.62}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249955 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250362 1069 "q-115214" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250362 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$ and $C$ are real constants, find the value of $A\\cdot B\\cdot C.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250362 1072 #uuid "1a9eca33-ce1c-5c81-8c1b-83778e94422b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250362 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-2)^{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+9 & =A(x-2)^{2}+B(x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2}-6(2)+9 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2-2)+C \\\\ 1 & =A(0)+B(0)+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-6(0)+9 & =A(0-2)^{2}+B(0-2)+1 \\\\ 9 & =4A-2B+1 \\\\ 0 & =4A-2B-8 \\\\ 0 & =2A-B-4 \\\\ B & =2A-4.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x=1$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}-6(1)+9 & =A(1-2)^{2}+B(1-2)+1 \\\\ 4 & =A-B+1 \\\\ 0 & =A-B-3 \\\\ B & =A-3.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A-4 & =A-3 \\\\ 2A-A & =-3+4 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $A$ into $(2)$  to get the value of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =A-3=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B\\cdot C & =1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250018 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250363 1069 "q-115215" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250363 1071 "Solve  $6{x}^{2}-7<5{x}^{2}+9.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250363 1072 #uuid "f9bf5fff-6a96-5072-a875-8ef7cab3f937" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250363 1074 "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-7 & <5{x}^{2}+9 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & <9+7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & <16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & <16 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{16} \\\\ |x| & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have $-4<x<4.$\n\nSo, the solution is $\\,x\\in (-4,4).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}<16$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-16<0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y={x}^{2}-16$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 4.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-16$ is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution to the given inequality ($y<0$) lies between the roots (but does not include them).\n\n![](images/48/48ffe19b490220651ddba5095ae192c49df2e36d1b203031784fd8c3f4aaa559.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-4,4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249992 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250364 1069 "q-115217" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250364 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}-7x+8}{(x-1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-1)^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$ and $C$ are real constants, find the value of $A\\cdot B\\cdot C.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250364 1072 #uuid "3129fa9e-b51c-5508-adb0-e28c2d24226f" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250364 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-1)^{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-7x+8 & =A(x-1)^{2}+B(x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=1$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-7(1)+8 & =A(1-1)^{2}+B(1-1)+C \\\\ 4 & =A(0)+B(0)+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)^{2}-7(0)+8 & =A(0-1)^{2}+B(0-1)+4 \\\\ 8 & =A-B+4 \\\\ 0 & =A-B-4 \\\\ B & =A-4.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x=2$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)^{2}-7(2)+8 & =A(2-1)^{2}+B(2-1)+4 \\\\ 6 & =A+B+4 \\\\ 0 & =A+B-2 \\\\ B & =2-A.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4 & =2-A \\\\ 2A & =6 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $A$ into $(2)$  to get the value of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =3-4=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B\\cdot C & =3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 4=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249689 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250365 1069 "q-115218" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250365 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1}\\le \\frac{1}{9}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250365 1072 #uuid "9725f40a-ccc5-5b84-9193-a09e8482b8d4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250365 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ (x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ (x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ (x+1)(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le -\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & \\ge \\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249877 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250366 1069 "q-115222" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250366 1071 "Solve the inequality ${(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x+6}+1\\ge 2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250366 1072 #uuid "ac4b46ad-428b-596b-a6dd-aa26aa057694" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250366 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x+6}+1 & \\ge 2 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x+6} & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x+6}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\ (x+6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+6)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+6)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6) & \\le \\frac{0}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ x+6 & \\le 0 \\\\ x & \\le -6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249995 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250367 1069 "q-115223" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250367 1071 "Solve  $7{x}^{2}>5{x}^{2}+14.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250367 1072 #uuid "492a7b9d-ed81-5bca-ba1d-9f7a16a3ffc4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250367 1074 "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{2} & >5{x}^{2}+14 \\\\ 7{x}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & >14 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & >14 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & >7 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{7} \\\\ |x| & >\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx<-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{7})\\cup (\\sqrt{7},\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}>7$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y={x}^{2}-7$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =7 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{7}.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-7$ is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside the roots (but does not include them).\n\n![](images/1e/1eba3bc41624ea6fdd11468b53630fd8995541ff01548ec57a36dad271e375f8.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{7})\\cup (\\sqrt{7},\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249828 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250368 1069 "q-115227" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250368 1071 "Solve the inequality $2\\cdot {6}^{x+1}+4<1.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250368 1072 #uuid "bdf9d7dd-1b44-5510-b773-7e7235d003d4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250368 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {6}^{x+1}+4 & <1 \\\\ 2\\cdot {6}^{x+1} & <-3 \\\\ {6}^{x+1} & <-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $6>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{6}^{x+1}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nTherefore, ${6}^{x+1}$ cannot be less than $-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Hence, the inequality\n\n$$\n{6}^{x+1}<-\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nhas no solutions." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250097 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250369 1069 "q-115230" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250369 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}(0.72)^{3x-7}+2\\le -4.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250369 1072 #uuid "8c4c4542-894b-5890-9a4c-cece675d50b3" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250369 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(0.72)^{3x-7}+2 & \\le -4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0.72)^{3x-7} & \\le -6 \\\\ (0.72)^{3x-7} & \\le -12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n(0.72)^{3x-7}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $(0.72)^{3x-7}\\le -12$ has no solutions." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249729 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250370 1069 "q-115231" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250370 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4>-2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250370 1072 #uuid "ced4ffe4-e7bc-5404-a238-528823539017" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250370 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4 & >-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5} & >-6 \\\\ (0.43)^{4x+5} & >-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n(0.43)^{4x+5}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $(0.43)^{4x+5}>-4$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249723 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250371 1069 "q-115233" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250371 1071 "Solve the inequality $2{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7}+1<-5.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250371 1072 #uuid "ef8ebb72-c9f4-522b-b5c8-2cd601e5dcba" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250371 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7}+1 & <-5 \\\\ 2{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7} & <-6 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7} & <-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $\\frac{1}{3}>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nTherefore, ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7}$ cannot be less than $-3.$ Hence, the inequality\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x+7}<-3\n$$\n\nhas no solutions." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249888 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250372 1069 "q-115236" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250372 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}<-2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250372 1072 #uuid "edab32df-2494-5eb8-99f7-875072c0dc5d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250372 1074 "We can solve this inequality using either of the two methods described below.\n\n**Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality**\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero. Therefore, it is impossible for the square of a real number to be smaller than $-2.$\n\nSo, the inequality ${x}^{2}<-2$ has no solutions.\n\n**Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve**\n\nLet's bring all the terms to the left-hand side,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2<0,\n$$\n\nand then plot the parabola $y={x}^{2}+2.$ The desired inequality ($y<0$) is never satisfied, so there are no solutions.\n\n![](images/cd/cd3ced552ef08804a308a47c7e1fbc5be9b045ee6a79203ba5e88f5a38a130ab.png)" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250042 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250373 1069 "q-115238" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250373 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+17x+8}{x(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+2}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{D}{(x+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$ are real constants, determine the value of $AB+CD.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250373 1072 #uuid "fa8af3a7-3c6c-5ee5-a9d6-11c834b852be" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250373 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $x(x+2)^{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+17x+8=A(x+2)^{3}+Bx(x+2)^{2}+Cx(x+2)+Dx.\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{3}+8(-2)^{2}+17(-2)+8 & =A(-2+2)^{3}+B(-2)(-2+2)^{2}+C(-2)(-2+2)+D(-2) \\\\ -8+32-34+8 & =-2D \\\\ -8+32-34+8 & =-2D \\\\ -2 & =-2D \\\\ D & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{3}+8(0)^{2}+17(0)+8 & =A(0+2)^{3}+B(0)(0+2)^{2}+C(0)(0+2)+D(0) \\\\ 8 & =8A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-1$ and using our values for $A$ and $D$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{3}+8(-1)^{2}+17(-1)+8 & =1(-1+2)^{3}+B(-1)(-1+2)^{2}+C(-1)(-1+2)+1(-1) \\\\ -1+8-17+8 & =1-B-C-1 \\\\ B & =2-C.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x=-3$ and using our values for $A$ and $D$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)^{3}+8(-3)^{2}+17(-3)+8 & =1(-3+2)^{3}+B(-3)(-3+2)^{2}+C(-3)(-3+2)+1(-3) \\\\ -27+72-51+8 & =-1-3B+3C-3 \\\\ 3B & =3C-6 \\\\ B & =C-2.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-C & =C-2 \\\\ -2C & =-4 \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $C$ into $(1)$  to get the value of $B:$\n\n$$\nB=2-2=0\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nAB+CD=1(0)+2(1)=2.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250044 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250374 1069 "q-115248" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250374 1071 "Solve the inequality ${7}^{x+1}>{5}^{x-1}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250374 1072 #uuid "7dea1588-01f3-5e0d-9811-784ff06fa34a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250374 1074 "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{x+1} & >{5}^{x-1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{x+1}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x-1}) \\\\ (x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & >(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & >x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x & >-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249682 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250375 1069 "q-115249" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250375 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+1}{(x-1)(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{x-2}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{D}{(x-2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$ are real constants, determine the value of $AB-CD.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250375 1072 #uuid "2b3cae04-fc2c-5d4b-a480-41491c80bb7d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250375 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-1)(x-2)^{3}$ to give\n\n$$\n2x+1=A(x-2)^{3}+B(x-1)(x-2)^{2}+C(x-1)(x-2)+D(x-1).\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)+1 & =A(2-2)^{3}+B(2-1)(2-2)^{2}+C(2-1)(2-2)+D(2-1) \\\\ 4+1 & =D \\\\ D & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=1$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)+1 & =A(1-2)^{3}+B(1-1)(1-2)^{2}+C(1-1)(1-2)+D(1-1) \\\\ 2+1 & =-A \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our values for $A$ and $D$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+1 & =-3(0-2)^{3}+B(0-1)(0-2)^{2}+C(0-1)(0-2)+5(0-1) \\\\ 0+1 & =24-4B+2C-5 \\\\ 2C & =4B-18 \\\\ C & =2B-9.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x=3$ and using our values for $A$ and $D$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)+1 & =-3(3-2)^{3}+B(3-1)(3-2)^{2}+C(3-1)(3-2)+5(3-1) \\\\ 6+1 & =-3+2B+2C+10 \\\\ 2C & =-2B \\\\ C & =-B.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2B-9 & =-B \\\\ 3B & =9 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $B$ into $(2)$  to get the value of $C:$\n\n$$\nC=-3\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nAB-CD=-3(3)-(-3)(5)=6.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249881 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250376 1069 "q-115250" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250376 1071 "Solve the inequality ${5}^{x-1}<{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250376 1072 #uuid "1c08f0a1-4fee-59ab-b31f-f6a9816dc7a4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250376 1074 "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x-1} & <{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x-1}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x}] \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(2-x)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249870 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250377 1069 "q-115255" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250377 1071 "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250377 1072 #uuid "43f2610d-716b-501f-9efa-0f7a8487b469" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250377 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{2}}{1+(-x)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249939 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250378 1069 "q-115256" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250378 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250378 1072 #uuid "339c9ddd-1427-5010-afc0-388d00dca36c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250378 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-x)^{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6}) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249987 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250379 1069 "q-115258" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250379 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250379 1072 #uuid "f2781105-5095-50e2-90f4-0da2296af6b1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250379 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250087 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250380 1069 "q-115259" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250380 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250380 1072 #uuid "9352396d-e883-546f-89b0-31aa842bd344" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250380 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-x)^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250084 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250381 1069 "q-115263" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250381 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250381 1072 #uuid "05da4bb4-efa5-5a74-9032-2827a097e82b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250381 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)}{1+(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250056 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250382 1069 "q-115269" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250382 1071 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-3,-4)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-4,-2).$ If ${f}^{′}(-3)=3,$ estimate the value of $f(-2.9)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-3$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250382 1072 #uuid "b0f105af-5e49-531a-9bde-15dfe8a93d65" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250382 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-3)+{f}^{′}(-3)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(-3,-4),$ so we have $f(-3)=-4.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(-3)=3,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-4+3(x+3) \\\\  & =-4+3x+9 \\\\  & =3x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-2.9)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-2.9.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2.9) & ≈L(-2.9) \\\\  & =3(-2.9)+5 \\\\  & =-8.7+5 \\\\  & =-3.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-4,-2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=-3,$ and therefore we have an overestimate." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250101 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250383 1069 "q-115270" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250383 1071 "Solve the inequality $5{x}^{2}+2x>2x-3.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250383 1072 #uuid "216e7771-39e5-53d6-a8ca-5a44a832ae71" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250383 1074 "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2x & >2x-3 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x-2x & >2x-3-2x \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & >-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n**Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality**\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero.\n\nSo, the inequality is true for every real number. Therefore the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\n**Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve**\n\nStarting from $5{x}^{2}>-3$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+3>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y=5{x}^{2}+3.$ :\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $5{x}^{2}+3$   is positive, the parabola opens upward.  The desired inequality $(y>0)$ is always satisfied. Therefore, the solution set to the given inequality includes all real numbers. That is, $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/60/60aede2dc3b2823aecd1e808210f96ffe5c6430d09a391fe6f4cf0e88a96e786.png)" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249894 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250384 1069 "q-115274" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250384 1071 "![](images/40/402ae2f9f3d1e0ce970a18b68332400d261962e40ebde9acdc179727e94f32ac.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality  $(x+1)(x-4)>0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250384 1072 #uuid "4cc14306-9d6a-5810-a2fa-5b835eb7c100" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250384 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-4)>0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=(x+1)(x-4)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/98/98537a1ab5afd590c47a2605d6d0bc8858f51e838033cc61a38845b3e8c87a53.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-1$ or $x>4.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249807 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250385 1069 "q-115275" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250385 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250385 1072 #uuid "c52ca68f-33c1-5cf6-9bde-bf78ae92f92f" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250385 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{5}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral I is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}+(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(-x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249925 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250386 1069 "q-115276" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250386 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250386 1072 #uuid "502954f5-6773-593e-9d60-d2c07c09bac0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250386 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249754 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250387 1069 "q-115278" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250387 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250387 1072 #uuid "fd35b4dd-cb0b-59b9-9a08-35fd436a6a59" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250387 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[3]{-x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250020 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250388 1069 "q-115283" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250388 1071 "![](images/3c/3cb42ff631a185d330d304e1e75a9346d137db066483946e99fddf21865718ef.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $10+3x-{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250388 1072 #uuid "81702de0-1941-5301-9aae-d993fbe2a53c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250388 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n10+3x-{x}^{2}\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=10+3x-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b8/b85abe0f3c20d50c856d7f73a1b0afc82c637e8afc258d2c689801a44e5ea4a5.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-2\\le x\\le 5.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250024 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250389 1069 "q-115285" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250389 1071 "![](images/0e/0e0941c1d165feb5378d73b69d2fc7bc59a0869e83f78ec064bfb929e1ca21d4.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(x+1)(x+6)>0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250389 1072 #uuid "27221316-39e8-5da5-850e-1444647b4eeb" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250389 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x+6)>0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=(x+1)(x+6)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ae/ae41c9e159852d1b64f3afce5c27ecf7202afd069f33936e7399679edabe4d97.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-6$ or $x>-1.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249790 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250390 1069 "q-115286" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250390 1071 "![](images/07/07003c76b7939be373eb4b1dc810e721f44b8c0405ee5312fd211f3b9959634b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250390 1072 #uuid "390b2e5b-addf-5887-a46a-a83def8e8ae2" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250390 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-4,3),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=4,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250099 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250391 1069 "q-115287" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250391 1071 "![](images/bb/bb57d377a05a6e169c32f2442e02c3926391d62bff7cb24cf3201fb0289b59a4.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $7-6x-{x}^{2}>0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250391 1072 #uuid "8e9f024f-9fc0-58af-9612-031b9867fc38" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250391 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n7-6x-{x}^{2}>0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola  \n\n$$\ny=7-6x-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e12975187d46b6dc408e21234309e33654a24503db65ed075e32990de8ed654.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-7<x<1.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249851 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250392 1069 "q-115293" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250392 1071 "![](images/96/9699c059d44d63cac608c76e130381139973a9d1579b4703801ec9b99f4e5e6e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250392 1072 #uuid "16e151d0-3971-5ce7-9114-fffc68511b51" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250392 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(4,5),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249700 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250393 1069 "q-115294" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250393 1071 "![](images/bd/bdfab2c7bdab1c3b360a0be55507ace6198b005f944fb8c7f2928bb5943d337c.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250393 1072 #uuid "6fc68743-3221-56b6-828a-93eeeb3dbbdf" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250393 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(2,4),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249714 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250394 1069 "q-115297" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250394 1071 "![](images/10/1006eabe3321beb48df2cd936ff2252b77e8ded81f9c5b7aec4d143e43941088.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250394 1072 #uuid "e17dda64-b9d6-527b-acc5-6bdc5ef17fe0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250394 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-4,0),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250005 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250395 1069 "q-115307" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250395 1071 "What is the center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{16}=1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250395 1072 #uuid "8f8934d3-0ed3-5c05-9140-f4dd98784b19" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250395 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=4,$ so the center is located at $(2,4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249832 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250396 1069 "q-115308" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250396 1071 "What is the center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250396 1072 #uuid "1dd86485-ed9c-5475-8932-90607bb9958e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250396 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-2$ and $k=3,$ so the center is located at $(-2,3).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249869 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250397 1069 "q-115309" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250397 1071 "What is the center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{36}=1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250397 1072 #uuid "f54c6d5e-e4e3-567e-970f-b558dfc0293b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250397 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-4$ and $k=6,$ so the center is located at $(-4,6).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249738 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250398 1069 "q-11531" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250398 1071 "Calculate $3F+\\frac{1}{2}G$, where\n$F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$G=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250398 1072 #uuid "e51b6e2d-bb1c-588d-8de2-5ed3d6ef6bca" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250398 1074 "We multiply each entry of $F$ by $3$ and each entry of $G$ by $\\frac{1}{2},$ and then add the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3F+\\frac{1}{2}G & =3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 3 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-2) & 3\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+1 & 9+0 \\\\ -6+2 & 6+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250055 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250399 1069 "q-115310" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250399 1071 "What is the center of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{25}=1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250399 1072 #uuid "ac526dd6-515d-59f1-a82a-21b39db68f06" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250399 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=0$ and $k=-4,$ so the center is located at $(0,-4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249827 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250400 1069 "q-115312" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250400 1071 "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{3}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250400 1072 #uuid "41ce4980-70a8-5c02-a7a7-5f08eba21e7b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250400 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=4$ and $k=5,$ so the center is located at $(4,5).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=3.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=3,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249842 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250401 1069 "q-115314" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250401 1071 "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is $3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+36x+32y=-124.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250401 1072 #uuid "0b1c09a8-ecd6-5ac4-aed2-f9a5b10019aa" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250401 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+36x+32y & =-124 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+36x)+(4{y}^{2}+32y) & =-124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}+36x)+(4{y}^{2}+32y) & =-124 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+12x]+4[{y}^{2}+8y] & =-124 \\\\ 3[(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}]+4[(y+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-124 \\\\ 3[(x+6)^{2}-36]+4[(y+4)^{2}-16] & =-124 \\\\ 3(x+6)^{2}-108+4(y+4)^{2}-64 & =-124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+6)^{2}-108+4(y+4)^{2}-64 & =-124 \\\\ 3(x+6)^{2}+4(y+4)^{2} & =-124+108+64 \\\\ 3(x+6)^{2}+4(y+4)^{2} & =48 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(-6,-4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250106 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250402 1069 "q-115316" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250402 1071 "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is\n$2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+16x-2y+29=0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250402 1072 #uuid "abf43efd-2730-5511-9044-94688958afd8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250402 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+16x-2y+29 & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+16x)+({y}^{2}-2y)+29 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{2}+16x)+({y}^{2}-2y)+29 & =0 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}+8x]+[{y}^{2}-2y]+29 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}]+[(y-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+29 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x+4)^{2}-16]+[(y-1)^{2}-1]+29 & =0 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{2}-32+(y-1)^{2}-1+29 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)^{2}-32+(y-1)^{2}-1+29 & =0 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =32+1-29 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(-4,1).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249813 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250403 1069 "q-115323" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250403 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250403 1072 #uuid "804e099a-7eac-522b-b475-41496ab8d992" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250403 1074 "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n4x-10=A(x-2)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2)-10 & =A(2-2)+B \\\\ -2 & =0+B \\\\ B & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)-10 & =A(0-2)+B \\\\ -10 & =-2A-2 \\\\ -2A & =-8 \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{(x-2)}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x-10}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{(x-2)}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-2(-\\frac{1}{x-2})+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{2}{x-2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249684 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250404 1069 "q-115346" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250404 1071 "![](images/84/84a9754d9291ba38d6cd257056d20896ce2ff89b8ee98210c8789fc9d633ae5f.png)\n\nFind the coordinates $(h,k)$ of the center of the circle above given that the points $(-1,4)$ and $(-7,-4)$ lie on a diameter of the circle." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250404 1072 #uuid "032b9fca-ff0f-5251-a672-9ed3b0e3a341" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250404 1074 "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. Therefore, we can calculate it using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-1-7}{2},\\frac{4-4}{2}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{8}{2},\\frac{0}{2}) \\\\  & =(-4,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249789 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250405 1069 "q-115353" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250405 1071 "${\\int }_{2/3}^{2}\\frac{3}{(3x-2)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250405 1072 #uuid "3a17cc8d-0938-5ad1-ae94-e242754caed6" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250405 1074 "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=3x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2/3}^{2}\\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{(3x-2)^{5}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{4}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{(-\\frac{1}{4}\\,{u}^{-4})|}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}|}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{1}{256}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250036 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250406 1069 "q-115360" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250406 1071 "The points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-3,-4)$ and $(0,-4)$, respectively, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle. Find the radius of the circle." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250406 1072 #uuid "6fa395af-9563-5dd6-b788-298fc3edabb7" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250406 1074 "The diameter can be calculated using the distance formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3-0)^{2}+(-4-(-4))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is half the diameter:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{d}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249795 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250407 1069 "q-115404" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250407 1071 "Solve the inequality $(4{x}^{6}+3)\\cdot (0.33)^{2x-5}\\ge 0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250407 1072 #uuid "50c4e960-d251-5c74-8e6f-d7eba5ced7c9" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250407 1074 "When we have an inequality involving an exponential function with a positive base, we're allowed to multiply or divide both sides by the exponential function.\n\nDividing both sides of the given inequality by $(0.33)^{2x-5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{6}+3)\\cdot (0.33)^{2x-5} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(4{x}^{6}+3)\\cdot (0.33)^{2x-5}}{(0.33)^{2x-5}} & \\ge \\frac{0}{(0.33)^{2x-5}} \\\\ 4{x}^{6}+3 & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFurthermore, $4{x}^{6}+3\\ge 0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the solution is all real numbers." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249769 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250408 1069 "q-115408" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250408 1071 "Solve the inequality $(2x-5)^{3}\\cdot {3}^{x+6}<0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250408 1072 #uuid "991c8643-e241-50e5-8e59-a614cc62cd0c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250408 1074 "When we have an inequality involving an exponential function with a positive base, we're allowed to multiply or divide both sides by the exponential function.\n\nDividing both sides of the given inequality by ${3}^{x+6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-5)^{3}\\cdot {3}^{x+6} & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(2x-5)^{3}\\cdot {3}^{x+6}}{{3}^{x+6}} & <\\frac{0}{{3}^{x+6}} \\\\ (2x-5)^{3} & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{(2x-5)^{3}} & <\\sqrt[3]{0} \\\\ 2x-5 & <0 \\\\ 2x & <5 \\\\ x & <\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249751 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250409 1069 "q-115410" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250409 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{({x}^{2}-4)^{3}}{(0.6)^{3x-2}}<0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250409 1072 #uuid "8e9422d6-b5d0-523a-afb2-aabcc6793fd0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250409 1074 "When we have an inequality involving an exponential function with a positive base, we're allowed to multiply or divide both sides by the exponential function.\n\nMultiplying both sides of the given inequality by $(0.6)^{3x-2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4)^{3}}{(0.6)^{3x-2}} & <0 \\\\ (0.6)^{3x-2}\\cdot \\frac{({x}^{2}-4)^{3}}{(0.6)^{3x-2}} & <(0.6)^{3x-2}\\cdot 0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4)^{3} & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-4)^{3}} & <\\sqrt[3]{0} \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & <0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & <4 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{4} \\\\ |x| & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is $-2<x<2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249707 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250410 1069 "q-115415" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250410 1071 "![](images/52/52b283fd5a8d9f374fad0a007f127598b86c53d7acf4df05d1415589e43faaa0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250410 1072 #uuid "0dfebbb3-1e7f-58e9-a0ac-939acd360f4d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250410 1074 "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(3,1).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(3,1),$ so we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {3}^{2} & =4p(1) \\\\ 9 & =4p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249907 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250411 1069 "q-115420" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250411 1071 "![](images/41/410da7fdbf283aba57c2000a37c56a87e11ed1b1372183a1d3c808376dba495f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250411 1072 #uuid "913fe829-246c-5859-8570-cf82a68a6df8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250411 1074 "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-5,2).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-5,2),$ so we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-5)^{2} & =4p(2) \\\\ 25 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{25}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{25}{8})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250119 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250412 1069 "q-115426" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250412 1071 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+13x<0?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250412 1072 #uuid "f2bb17ed-6922-578d-aad8-d1768d7a7b6f" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250412 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+13x & <0 \\\\ x(x+13) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x+13)=0.$ The roots are $x=-13$ and $x=0,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-13),$ $(-13,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-13)$ | $(-13,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+13$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x+13)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x+13)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x+13)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-13,0).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+13x<0$ for all $x\\in (-13,0).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249752 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250413 1069 "q-115429" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250413 1071 "Solve the inequality $5x\\cdot {7}^{2x+3}+4\\cdot {7}^{2x+3}\\ge -2\\cdot {7}^{2x+3}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250413 1072 #uuid "bffeaf0e-fd8d-5920-b105-3b02ab9ca007" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250413 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\cdot {7}^{2x+3}+4\\cdot {7}^{2x+3} & \\ge -2\\cdot {7}^{2x+3} \\\\ 5x\\cdot {7}^{2x+3}+4\\cdot {7}^{2x+3}+2\\cdot {7}^{2x+3} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (5x+4+2){7}^{2x+3} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (5x+6){7}^{2x+3} & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${7}^{2x+3}$ and solve the resulting inequality using familiar techniques:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+6 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 5x & \\ge -6 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to the inequality above is $x\\ge -\\frac{6}{5}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249846 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250414 1069 "q-115430" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250414 1071 "Solve the inequality $4\\cdot {6}^{3x-2}>-{6}^{3x-2}-{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{3x-2}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250414 1072 #uuid "f57739ef-24a7-58da-b6c7-4934e820a3b3" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250414 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {6}^{3x-2} & >-{6}^{3x-2}-{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{3x-2} \\\\ 4\\cdot {6}^{3x-2}+{6}^{3x-2}+{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{3x-2} & >0 \\\\ (4+1+{x}^{2}){6}^{3x-2} & >0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+5){6}^{3x-2} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${6}^{3x-2}$ and solve the resulting inequality using familiar techniques:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+5){6}^{3x-2} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+5 & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${x}^{2}+5>0$ for all values of $x,$ the solution is all real numbers." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249927 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250415 1069 "q-115431" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250415 1071 "Solve the inequality $3x\\cdot {5}^{x+6}-{5}^{x+6}<8\\cdot {5}^{x+6}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250415 1072 #uuid "acf6ef6d-0331-57a6-8919-a62e7cb25503" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250415 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x\\cdot {5}^{x+6}-{5}^{x+6} & <8\\cdot {5}^{x+6} \\\\ 3x\\cdot {5}^{x+6}-{5}^{x+6}-8\\cdot {5}^{x+6} & <0 \\\\ (3x-1-8){5}^{x+6} & <0 \\\\ (3x-9){5}^{x+6} & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${5}^{x+6}$ and solve the resulting inequality using familiar techniques:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-9 & <0 \\\\ 3x & <9 \\\\ x & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to the inequality above is $x<3.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249701 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250416 1069 "q-115432" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250416 1071 "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(2,1).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250416 1072 #uuid "88252d6a-eb99-5de5-8ab4-de1f72848e55" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250416 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,1),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(1) \\\\ 4 & =4p \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(1)y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249818 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250417 1069 "q-115434" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250417 1071 "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-5,2).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250417 1072 #uuid "b0c85b4b-81a1-52fc-99a2-07f13ad067d3" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250417 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(-5,2),$ so we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-5)^{2} & =4p(2) \\\\ 25 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{25}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{25}{8})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249765 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250418 1069 "q-115435" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250418 1071 "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-1,-1).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250418 1072 #uuid "d48a1800-0baf-5dcd-a800-d0ee3d7aed53" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250418 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,-1),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=-1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-1)^{2} & =4p(-1) \\\\ 1 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249699 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250419 1069 "q-115438" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250419 1071 "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-4,-2).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250419 1072 #uuid "6fa219a9-5301-517d-a36d-5e921226eec4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250419 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(-2) \\\\ 16 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-2)y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-8y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250048 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250420 1069 "q-115450" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250420 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2x-7}\\ge {2}^{2x-3}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250420 1072 #uuid "fd846e2f-c905-5a86-be7e-e88bbb14f907" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250420 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2x-7} & \\ge {2}^{2x-3} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2x-7}-{2}^{2x-3} & \\ge 0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-{2}^{4})\\cdot {2}^{2x-7} & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${2}^{2x-7}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{2}^{4}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve this inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{2}^{4} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge 16 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{16} \\\\ |x| & \\ge 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -4$ or $x\\ge 4.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249740 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250421 1069 "q-115451" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250421 1071 "![](images/97/970db64a0ddd325993323511ad4ae754ce64f64d591877bfa0ac908874e2393f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250421 1072 #uuid "af2d74a7-36e3-5f6f-95f7-10b5d7e5decf" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250421 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-1).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(3-1) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =2(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250068 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250422 1069 "q-115458" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250422 1071 "Solve the inequality $x\\cdot {7}^{4x+2}<-{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{4x+1}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250422 1072 #uuid "f98b9f4b-7715-5d41-9b70-66159490208a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250422 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {7}^{4x+2} & <-{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{4x+1} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{4x+1}+x\\cdot {7}^{4x+2} & <0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{4x+1}+7x\\cdot {7}^{4x+1} & <0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+7x)\\cdot {7}^{4x+1} & <0 \\\\ x(x+7)\\cdot {7}^{4x+1} & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${7}^{4x+1}$ and get\n\n$$\nx(x+7)<0.\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=-7$ and $x=0.$ The parabola is below the $x$-axis when $-7<x<0.$\n\n![](images/b8/b884ee15f864bcd02639085dc1465a82958ef0faeb9408e68b405c5906ea3a18.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $-7<x<0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249746 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250423 1069 "q-115460" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250423 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{7x+2}\\le 2x\\cdot {3}^{7x+3}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250423 1072 #uuid "4c16a333-405a-54ec-8690-b3c58b202441" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250423 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{7x+2} & \\le 2x\\cdot {3}^{7x+3} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{7x+2}-2x\\cdot {3}^{7x+3} & \\le 0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x\\cdot 3)\\cdot {3}^{7x+2} & \\le 0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)\\cdot {3}^{7x+2} & \\le 0 \\\\ x(x-6)\\cdot {3}^{7x+2} & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by ${3}^{7x+2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx(x-6)\\le 0.\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=0$ and $x=6.$ The parabola is below the $x$-axis or touch this axis when $0\\le x\\le 6.$\n\n![](images/d7/d75b387ac3e54f253eab62d9492b39f2744298e776f663d542b26e3c47f7d6a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $0\\le x\\le 6.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249797 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250424 1069 "q-115500" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250424 1071 "![](images/e2/e26f3115e64d22d336c164fca762a1654ce515019f425f5b0b772276ca88f727.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250424 1072 #uuid "af0b97a8-b635-5432-9208-09a4d6d1e1c0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250424 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,4),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (1-4)^{2} & =4p(4) \\\\ 9 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{16})y \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249882 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250425 1069 "q-115502" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250425 1071 "![](images/c6/c6b336b55e48a68dcad83efc4a8acd7659225b8f8e29faf8ea176c2cead29901.