arabola also passes through the point $(2,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (2+1)^{2} & =4p(-2+5) \\\\ 9 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})(y+5) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =3(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249853 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250427 1069 "q-115509" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250427 1071 "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-3)$ and which passes through the point $(-1,2).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250427 1072 #uuid "92e783e4-7f06-5937-98d3-6bf3624a8108" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250427 1074 "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(1,-3).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-(-3)) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-1,2),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+3) \\\\ (-1-1)^{2} & =4p(2+3) \\\\ 4 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{5})(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249969 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [1759218625042