'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x]^{′}}{{[x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249954 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250430 1069 "q-115517" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250430 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250430 1072 #uuid "ef69b6da-6b79-5e57-a05c-34b900661ea4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250430 1074 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{x}]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{-{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]^{′}}{[x]^{′}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250031 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250431 1069 "q-115518" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250431 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250431 1072 #uuid "a4bbbb84-0fac-5f07-a8f5-38985b38226e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250431 1074 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)]^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}{(\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}){cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249800 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250432 1069 "q-115519" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250432 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x}{x+1}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250432 1072 #uuid "d4d575ad-474a-547c-88ad-41cb9bf33eb6" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250432 1074 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(x+1)=-\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{-\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x}{x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x]^{′}}{{[x+1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{1-x}-2)}{1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{1-x}+2) \\\\  & =-(0+2) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249768 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250433 1069 "q-115523" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250433 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250433 1072 #uuid "8d5a6bfe-2213-5fca-8027-6b27cb0a5f81" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250433 1074 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{tan}⁡x]^{′}}{{[{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249867 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250434 1069 "q-115526" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250434 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250434 1072 #uuid "f0b7651b-eacd-5715-8331-dab57a1d4302" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250434 1074 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}{-{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249771 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250435 1069 "q-115528" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250435 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250435 1072 #uuid "88fb5994-1227-5032-b75a-adafaeaf4ffe" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250435 1074 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}]^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x}{(\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249777 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250436 1069 "q-115530" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250436 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250436 1072 #uuid "e116ffff-24eb-5f64-952b-b5d8dd36f71b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250436 1074 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt{x}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{ln}^{2}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{(\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{8\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249717 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250437 1069 "q-115537" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250437 1071 "$\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250437 1072 #uuid "a2506c51-af3a-5dc3-a5a7-44e29ebb4d97" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250437 1074 "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{x(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{x(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-2}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(x-2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-12x+4=A(x-2)^{2}+Bx(x-2)+Cx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2)^{2}-12(2)+4 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2)(2-2)+C(2) \\\\ -4 & =2C \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}-12(0)+4 & =A(0-2)^{2}+B(0)(0-2)+C(0) \\\\ 4 & =4A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1)^{2}-12(1)+4 & =A(1-2)^{2}+B(1)(1-2)+C(1) \\\\ -4 & =A-B+C \\\\ -4 & =1-B+(-2) \\\\ -3 & =-B \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{3}{x-2}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-12x+4}{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{3}{x-2}-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\frac{2}{x-2}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249698 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250438 1069 "q-11556" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250438 1071 "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 0.5 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}1.5 & -3 & 0 & 1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0.2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\,\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250438 1072 #uuid "b4dac05e-84dd-5a26-905f-fb4d126dc4c6" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250438 1074 "- $P$ is *not* a square matrix since the numbers of rows and columns are not the same. It has $3$ rows and $2$ columns, so it's a $3\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- $S$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $2$ rows and $2$ columns, so it is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- $T$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $5$ rows and $5$ columns, so it is a $5\\times 5$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, only $S$ and $T$ are square matrices." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249902 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250439 1069 "q-115592" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250439 1071 "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1)$ and $f(1)={e}^{2}.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(\\frac{3}{4}).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250439 1072 #uuid "4bc1f703-1de2-59b6-981e-831238ea97de" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250439 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)={e}^{2},$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1),$ then ${f}^{′}(1)=2{e}^{2\\cdot 1}\\cdot 1(1+1)=4{e}^{2}.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(\\frac{3}{4})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249822 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250440 1069 "q-115601" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250440 1071 "![](images/49/4938d034c766b78e1da1a5bd19f2a12b314f772ca4819cd52d4f1463ff1ae3a4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250440 1072 #uuid "e94ede6e-b65e-547c-9e4b-813d5e9b97e0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250440 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(3,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(3,2),$ we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(3) \\\\ 4 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{3}$ is positive." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249879 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250441 1069 "q-115602" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250441 1071 "![](images/57/5746a438eccdd55d345f1c71a768f971aa1a342798743e151f7557102ddd8783.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250441 1072 #uuid "2b404c30-267f-56f0-958b-85883e368d93" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250441 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(1,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(1,-3),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-3)^{2} & =4p(1) \\\\ 9 & =4p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{9}{4}$ is positive." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250058 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250442 1069 "q-115603" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250442 1071 "![](images/b4/b472d30e6260b0e0f1ce4bc791c5ce3068d78a304711775e1327a4c3dbd8c3d3.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250442 1072 #uuid "a8aa75b6-dda5-58c9-ab6d-95e4d74e59b4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250442 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(2,-2),$ we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-2)^{2} & =4p(2) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{2}$ is positive." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249798 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250443 1069 "q-115608" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250443 1071 "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}(4x-9)$ and $f(1)=-2.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(1.5).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250443 1072 #uuid "3f055be2-bdc1-5b7a-ac96-2209c3dd80d8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250443 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)=-2,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}(4x-9),$ then ${f}^{′}(1)={1}^{2}(4\\cdot 1-9)=-5.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=-2-5(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.5)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.5) & ≈L(1.5) \\\\  & =-2-5(1.5-1) \\\\  & =-2-5(0.5) \\\\  & =-2-2.5 \\\\  & =-4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249887 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250444 1069 "q-115623" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250444 1071 "$\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250444 1072 #uuid "513d3507-74c5-5ea9-ac09-4c99e11dedbf" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250444 1074 "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{2x+1}+\\frac{B}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(2x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}=A(2x+1)^{2}+B(2x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =A{(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)}^{2}+B(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2} & =A(2(0)+1)^{2}+B(2(0)+1)+C \\\\ 0 & =A+B+1 \\\\ A+B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-1)^{2} & =A(2(-1)+1)^{2}+B(2(-1)+1)+C \\\\ 4 & =A-B+1 \\\\ A-B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=3$ and $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =3-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250016 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250445 1069 "q-115625" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250445 1071 "![](images/b1/b14a6063e0b1be751e738060087644cacaa3c3cf1e324ee66bf57232834e618d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250445 1072 #uuid "69e800ff-6717-5a96-b9a0-b74b69055c6e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250445 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-1,1).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,1),$ we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {1}^{2} & =4p(-1) \\\\ 1 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is negative." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249913 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250446 1069 "q-115628" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250446 1071 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at  $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(6,3).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250446 1072 #uuid "77a2fd6b-e9d0-50f6-a357-c099577e8b84" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250446 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(6,3),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {3}^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 9 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{8})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249950 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250447 1069 "q-11563" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250447 1071 "Which of the following best describes the following matrix $A?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250447 1072 #uuid "e9b78ccd-daa3-54ce-bf4c-d8fd41b5fce0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250447 1074 "The matrix has only one row. Hence, it is a row vector." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249801 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250448 1069 "q-115631" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250448 1071 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at  $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,-4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250448 1072 #uuid "4c98eb68-5485-54b6-b79b-f365d74db723" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250448 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(8,-4),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(8) \\\\ 16 & =32p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249780 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250449 1069 "q-115633" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250449 1071 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-2,-2).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250449 1072 #uuid "34ce73b1-4d1a-5e32-8244-a4c68ffbd052" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250449 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-2,-2),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-2)^{2} & =4p(-2) \\\\ 4 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250100 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250450 1069 "q-115634" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250450 1071 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-1,-4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250450 1072 #uuid "07da239d-b796-56b0-87bd-44779be7e860" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250450 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(-1) \\\\ 16 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-4)x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-16x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249974 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250451 1069 "q-115636" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250451 1071 "![](images/57/5742f3f1161782d06b8a785d28d6c59b1e40aec37bd6b62268d6d41e1c662ee0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250451 1072 #uuid "b141f773-2517-5672-ab61-0399ba3f3a79" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250451 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(-2,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-4,-4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-2,-1),$ we substitute $h=-2$ and $k=-1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-4$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+2) \\\\ (-4+1)^{2} & =4p(-4+2) \\\\ 9 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+2) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{9}{8})(x+2) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{9}{8}$ is negative." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249767 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250452 1069 "q-115640" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250452 1071 "![](images/c8/c88e54f2a7acec3815a53fa4b0d641dd370ccf708b876f8dd231f05e115ea6fc.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250452 1072 #uuid "dfe0d7b7-ce91-59ce-8e7a-ef17a494776b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250452 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(3,3)$ and passes through the point $(6,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,3),$ we substitute $h=3$ and $k=3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =4p(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(6,-3),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =4p(x-3) \\\\ (-3-3)^{2} & =4p(6-3) \\\\ 36 & =12p \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =4p(x-3) \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =4(3)(x-3) \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =12(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=3$ is positive." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249830 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250453 1069 "q-115642" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250453 1071 "![](images/8e/8ea1ad85f30978bed681f48e02f4af2f401a1f561390bee09683ead252fc1d7d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250453 1072 #uuid "fff1310e-72c2-5e26-9574-a4434ee14fd6" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250453 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(1,-2)$ and passes through the point $(4,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-2),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (-3+2)^{2} & =4p(4-1) \\\\ 1 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{12})(x-1) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{12}$ is positive." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249924 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250454 1069 "q-115643" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250454 1071 "![](images/1f/1f6330a6e9bc0e48326982c7b7509f3e2257de994c5736c813ede93a07491e70.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250454 1072 #uuid "30a0f072-9dc6-5517-92da-25f253588788" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250454 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,-3)$ and passes through the point $(3,0).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(0,-3),$ we substitute $h=0$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4px.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(3,0),$ so we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (0+3)^{2} & =4p(3) \\\\ 9 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})x \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ is positive." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249690 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250455 1069 "q-115644" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250455 1071 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-1)$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250455 1072 #uuid "4025b5ab-d584-549e-8e48-80f0cd57c2e9" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250455 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-1),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,1),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (1+1)^{2} & =4p(2-1) \\\\ 4 & =4p \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(1)(x-1) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249806 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250456 1069 "q-115646" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250456 1071 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-3)$ and passes through the point $(3,0).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250456 1072 #uuid "afe09fb4-eb0a-5d67-bf21-bab7a68c96cd" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250456 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-3),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(3,0),$ so we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (0+3)^{2} & =4p(3-1) \\\\ 9 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{8})(x-1) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249979 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250457 1069 "q-115648" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250457 1071 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,2)$ and passes through the point $(5,5).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250457 1072 #uuid "909b1cd7-0685-5536-88b4-2736b32e6e6d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250457 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(0,2),$ we substitute $h=0$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4px.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(5,5),$ so we can substitute $x=5$ and $y=5$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (5-2)^{2} & =4p(5) \\\\ 9 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{20})x \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{5}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249679 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250458 1069 "q-115649" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250458 1071 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,0)$ and passes through the point $(0,-2).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250458 1072 #uuid "c6a6caca-6d57-513d-aceb-bcb508f5faff" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250458 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(2,0),$ we substitute $h=2$ and $k=0$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=-2$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (-2)^{2} & =4p(0-2) \\\\ 4 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-2) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-2(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249773 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250459 1069 "q-115651" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250459 1071 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(-2,6).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250459 1072 #uuid "611aec54-1623-5eed-97bf-5fece3e940a8" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250459 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ we substitute $h=-1$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-2,6),$ so we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+1) \\\\ (6-2)^{2} & =4p(-2+1) \\\\ 16 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+1) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(-4)(x+1) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-16(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249982 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250460 1069 "q-115655" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250460 1071 "$\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250460 1072 #uuid "17b68d5d-09c0-504c-99d5-2bccfb24d4f6" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250460 1074 "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-6}+\\frac{B}{(x-6)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-6)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-6)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+9x-16=A(x-6)^{2}+B(x-6)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(6)^{2}+9(6)-16 & =A(6-6)^{2}+B(6-6)+C \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=5$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(5)^{2}+9(5)-16 & =A(5-6)^{2}+B(5-6)+C \\\\ 4 & =A-B+2 \\\\ A-B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=7$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(7)^{2}+9(7)-16 & =A(7-6)^{2}+B(7-6)+C \\\\ -2 & =A+B+2 \\\\ A+B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=2$ and $A+B=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =2-4 \\\\ A & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nand substituting back into $A+B=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+B & =-4 \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-5)^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{3}{(x-6)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x-6)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}+9x-16}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{3}{(x-6)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x-6)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x-6}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{(x-6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|+\\frac{3}{x-6}-\\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249784 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250461 1069 "q-115656" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250461 1071 "$\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250461 1072 #uuid "031d8c13-fb73-52e6-afed-79556c97e9a1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250461 1074 "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{3x+1}+\\frac{B}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(3x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+1=A(3x+1)^{2}+B(3x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+1 & =A{(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+1)}^{2}+B(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+1)+C \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(0)^{2}+1 & =A(3(0)+1)^{2}+B(3(0)+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =A+B+2 \\\\ A+B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(-1)^{2}+1 & =A(3(-1)+1)^{2}+B(3(-1)+1)+C \\\\ 10 & =4A-2B+2 \\\\ 4A-2B & =8 \\\\ 2A-B & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $2A-B=4$ and $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =4-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{3x+1}-\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3x+1}-\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249923 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250462 1069 "q-115662" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250462 1071 "$\\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250462 1072 #uuid "6058468b-fca6-572c-b924-db85b67482b1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250462 1074 "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+8}+\\frac{B}{(x+8)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+8)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+8)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\nx-2=A(x+8)^{2}+B(x+8)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-8,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-8)-2 & =A(-8+8)^{2}+B(-8+8)+C \\\\ -10 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=-7$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7)-2 & =A(-7+8)^{2}+B(-7+8)+C \\\\ -9 & =A+B-10 \\\\ A+B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-9$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)-2 & =A(-9+8)^{2}+B(-9+8)+C \\\\ -11 & =A-B-10 \\\\ A-B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=-1$ and $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =-1+1 \\\\ 2A & =0 \\\\ A & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+B & =1 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{(x+8)^{2}}-\\frac{10}{(x+8)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+8)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(x+8)^{2}}-\\frac{10}{(x+8)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+8)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-10\\int \\frac{1}{(x+8)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+8}+\\frac{5}{(x+8)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250113 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250463 1069 "q-115665" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250463 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+5}-\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250463 1072 #uuid "1b1b65b0-769f-5dc5-a09c-d74cd95adc54" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250463 1074 "First, we find the constant $B.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x-2 & =(2x+B)(x+1)-({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+5(0)-2 & =(2(0)+B)(0+1)-({0}^{2}+5) \\\\ -2 & =B-5 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x+3}{{x}^{2}+5}-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+5=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249770 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250464 1069 "q-115667" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250464 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{D}{x+2},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250464 1072 #uuid "4fcac2c1-8de9-5c0d-89db-ccf7066e1090" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250464 1074 "First, we find the constant $D.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+20 & =(x-2)(x+2)+D({x}^{2}+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+20 & =(0-2)(0+2)+D({0}^{2}+8) \\\\ 20 & =-4+8D \\\\ 24 & =8D \\\\ D & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{3}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+8=u.$ Then, $x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\text{d}x=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249774 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250465 1069 "q-115672" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250465 1071 "![](images/a9/a9c84c00dfdd0f84e8bf81dc4be8808794685a884f0e6bebd908da461e3de9a4.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250465 1072 #uuid "85fc7399-05ab-50ed-9612-91716f573915" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250465 1074 "![](images/d7/d7f1fccf2bb73f160faa04c058db6892f78f105e374516c97558f81121fc49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}(2-x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the three remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(2-x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(2-x).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249750 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250466 1069 "q-115673" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250466 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+20}-\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250466 1072 #uuid "aa383850-ed95-5745-b056-448c57db2176" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250466 1074 "First, we find the constant $B.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x-27 & =(2x+B)(x+1)-({x}^{2}+20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-5(0)-27 & =(2(0)+B)(0+1)-({0}^{2}+20) \\\\ -27 & =B-20 \\\\ B & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-7}{{x}^{2}+20}-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x-7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+20=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249833 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250467 1069 "q-115675" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250467 1071 "![](images/0d/0d1dba03b8b14af89dbf06bf84f02c1a15f4f47f230900d4c46de87f14baeaf9.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250467 1072 #uuid "b02352a2-ffeb-5da1-be23-2cbcaa163428" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250467 1074 "![](images/af/afb50fc9034d42ae57b9861dec4a2f5d0acf4f0c7122fc1ce845f5e3d60988fe.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(1-x)(x-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=1$ and $x=4$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<1$ | $1<x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the three remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249792 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250468 1069 "q-115677" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250468 1071 "![](images/b1/b16e7007b3a266291fa22c1760e55e5b5e0a3e9a2d4ea97303f379a1f372cd32.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250468 1072 #uuid "67ab633f-5765-5331-9702-c5a5e5a97d16" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250468 1074 "![](images/d8/d84c6743bd4cc1ab3306951f507f4691ea3c3f2075ead5f04328f64254d5a786.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(x+3) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x+1)(x+3) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x+3).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ it also evaluates to zero when $x=-1,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(x+3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=x(x+3).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250021 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250469 1069 "q-11568" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250469 1071 "Find $ST$,  if  $S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $T=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250469 1072 #uuid "9c07fc8a-7bd0-54d4-b86d-f5ce5f6701b1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250469 1074 "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ST & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+0+8 & 2+0-4 & 3+0+4 \\\\ 0-5+4 & 0+5-2 & 0-0+2 \\\\ 6+0+4 & 4-0-2 & 6+0+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249809 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250470 1069 "q-115680" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250470 1071 "![](images/ac/ac782575d30a0f87e5a930ae03ef91f9c606cc03baa5b4ff9037f871a63fccd9.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250470 1072 #uuid "27a787ca-e935-59fd-a10b-d5e3b5fdef5a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250470 1074 "![](images/f2/f2ad5a7c6ea5a224583246a32e0da6de66c834f4d87be1b5bc646d3c7a678163.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{2}(4-x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x+2)(x-4)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3x(x+2)(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=3x(x+2)(x-4)^{2}.$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=4,$ it also evaluates to zero when $x=0,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=4$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-2$ | $-2<x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{2}(4-x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{2}(4-x).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249779 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250471 1069 "q-115686" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250471 1071 "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250471 1072 #uuid "54337009-4b4f-594c-8090-7c81fe847987" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250471 1074 "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+1$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{C}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+1)(x-4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-9x+5 & =(Ax+B)(x-4)+C({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)} & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250076 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250472 1069 "q-115690" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250472 1071 "Find the integral of $\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250472 1072 #uuid "412d22df-d958-56dd-8884-22116d9638ff" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250472 1074 "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+6$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+6}+\\frac{C}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+6)(x-3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x & =(Ax+B)(x-3)+C({x}^{2}+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=-4,$ and $C=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)} & =\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}+6}-\\frac{2}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}+6}-\\frac{2}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+6| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249814 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250473 1069 "q-115693" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250473 1071 "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250473 1072 #uuid "b36c8e07-4439-57ae-b9d1-276ef485c6ec" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250473 1074 "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+3$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+3)(x+4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+5x-9 & =(Ax+B)(x+4)+C({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-3,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+3| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249837 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250474 1069 "q-115705" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250474 1071 "A right Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-1$ and $x=1$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *overestimate* of the area?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250474 1072 #uuid "fb7c51eb-42c7-51a3-9c3b-b86689289402" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250474 1074 "The right Riemann sum overestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is increasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only function that is increasing over $x\\in [-1,1]$ is as follows.\n\n![](images/d0/d0e535f39c947e55b6dba151f723dd3390fe67c29a571b940294e90869acac21.png)" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250043 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250475 1069 "q-115710" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250475 1071 "Evaluate the integral  $\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250475 1072 #uuid "74feace7-d730-58bc-b03e-4652e0e5c170" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250475 1074 "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD=(4)^{2}-4(1)(-6)=40\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{40}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x({x}^{2}+4x-6),$ and we get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+12x-12=A({x}^{2}+4x-6)+(Bx+C)x\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=2$, and $C=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}=\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+4x-6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(2x+4)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249858 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250476 1069 "q-115712" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250476 1071 "Evaluate the integral  $\\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250476 1072 #uuid "7d0e590b-4ba3-5c1f-9a3a-31d9c817ed33" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250476 1074 "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD=(5)^{2}-4(2)(-6)=73\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{73}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6),$ and we get\n\n$$\n12-5x=A(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)+(Bx+C)x\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-2,B=4$, and $C=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}=-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u=2{x}^{2}+5x-6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(4x+5)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250052 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250477 1069 "q-115714" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250477 1071 "![](images/36/36493e520e5f4dbc91482ab578ec4499eb00aee948171a6ea43d9d40c0e62938.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=-(x-a)(x-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250477 1072 #uuid "36dd5955-de9b-5451-a334-56eabc65f37a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250477 1074 "![](images/1d/1da6182ac4999453b1c7e5f4c7db4d0a7e42f285463e8fe35aac5b8df46eb400.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-(x+2)(x-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=4$ and $a+b=2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249958 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250478 1069 "q-115716" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250478 1071 "![](images/a0/a0b39135b78cefe8471c7d120850dde9b53786342679b5ed7f25af9110350607.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-x(x-3)$\n- ${y}^{′}=9-{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)(x-3)$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250478 1072 #uuid "1598c957-f6d6-5194-a4fb-4b15b46e8010" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250478 1074 "![](images/a4/a43217f2af81c1bbacbebd8800b7ebcd4076a32d6ff61782e1fdab4cd182d5ee.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-x(x-3)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=9-{x}^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=3,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=2,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249705 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250479 1069 "q-115719" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250479 1071 "![](images/17/170ddea2148f369d4feedd14540f234d7946bb6670d1ca6eb4c4ebbbb086a760.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+7)$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x+3)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+5)$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250479 1072 #uuid "c861b6c2-4257-5ae6-8d33-89c2fd430076" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250479 1074 "![](images/cf/cf77e09457cd6b53da801a6eb876a79712935eba623c851a5b6dafa58887a076.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=-7,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+7)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-7$ and $x=-3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at $x=-7,$ and also evaluates to zero when $x=0,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+5)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=-5,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249775 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250480 1069 "q-11572" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250480 1071 "Find $MN$, if  $M=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $N=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250480 1072 #uuid "8354208b-a3db-513f-9f3d-79cce970f22a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250480 1074 "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MN & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+0+4 & 4+0+2 & 6+0+4 \\\\ 0-0+4 & 0-1+2 & 0-0+4 \\\\ 1+0+4 & 2+0+2 & 3+0+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250078 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250481 1069 "q-115736" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250481 1071 "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-2,4].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $22.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250481 1072 #uuid "3db867c0-86fb-5b36-917d-09a549290c15" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250481 1074 "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nLet's set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+5+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+8)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $22$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+16 & =22 \\\\ 2k & =22-16 \\\\ k & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249854 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250482 1069 "q-115737" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250482 1071 "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-6,3].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $6.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $7$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $k$ |" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250482 1072 #uuid "da83e2f0-858c-5c9b-8c7c-d0635d7e101a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250482 1074 "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-3)+f(0)+f(3)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nLet's set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(5+(-2)+k)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(k+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3k+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $6$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k+9 & =6 \\\\ 3k & =6-9 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-3}{3} \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250086 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250483 1069 "q-115751" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250483 1071 "![](images/25/2552222c6a0be1b1bf1e67a66b4906bb735c2d974d724d3e25e988d15388c696.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{x+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250483 1072 #uuid "988f2d4b-1d26-5c62-baed-2061d6f81ef4" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250483 1074 "![](images/eb/eb1303132a4fe6a26c85b4733a3039a52109b4c49408e1c82038d71885d27f70.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side approaches $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -1,$ but it also approaches $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to 1,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{x+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{8}{x+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}=-\\frac{x}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})}\\to ∓\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250032 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250484 1069 "q-115754" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250484 1071 "![](images/34/34af8d1078100aea331c300ea529d2fdefbdd31e99ee8a7946325d58a8abf680.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=7-{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+7}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{x+7}$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250484 1072 #uuid "3be4e44a-f534-5d30-81ef-fc22fbf18076" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250484 1074 "![](images/d0/d02883b776dbcd55c5c96347ed1a7bb0e51ef8c26e62786049b6585b878d04fb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-7.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=7-{x}^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -7.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+7}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x+7}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(1+\\frac{7}{x})}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{x+7}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{x+7}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249785 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250485 1069 "q-115772" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250485 1071 "Given that $r=\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=7$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250485 1072 #uuid "734835be-6e59-529d-977d-cd321f0badd2" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250485 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=7$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250061 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250486 1069 "q-115776" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250486 1071 "Given that $r=-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=12$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250486 1072 #uuid "b468347a-5af4-5e0d-add1-8cb76b34a17b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250486 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=12$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (24\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})) \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (24\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{16} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249675 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250487 1069 "q-115788" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250487 1071 "Solve for $m$ where $\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}}=\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250487 1072 #uuid "1b056bc9-1b86-5285-9289-f5246eedec51" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250487 1074 "First, we note that $m=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by ${m}^{3},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}} & =\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}} \\\\ {m}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}}) & ={m}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}}) \\\\ {m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+{m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}} & ={m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}} \\\\ \\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{3m-1}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}}+\\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{m+2}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}} & =\\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2m+3}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}} \\\\ 3m-1+m+2 & =2m+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3m-1+m+2 & =2m+3 \\\\ 4m+1 & =2m+3 \\\\ 4m-2m & =3-1 \\\\ 2m & =2 \\\\ m & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=1\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=1.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249912 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250488 1069 "q-115791" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250488 1071 "Solve for $z$ where $\\frac{z+1}{2z}=2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250488 1072 #uuid "cb3909a1-5d95-5bcb-832e-e8e09e292f04" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250488 1074 "First, we note that $z=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $2z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z+1}{2z} & =2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z} \\\\ 2z\\cdot (\\frac{z+1}{2z}) & =2z\\cdot (2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z}) \\\\ 2z\\cdot \\frac{z+1}{2z} & =2z\\cdot 2-2z\\cdot \\frac{2z+1}{2z} \\\\ \\cancel{2z}\\cdot \\frac{z+1}{\\cancel{2z}} & =2z\\cdot 2-\\cancel{2z}\\cdot \\frac{2z+1}{\\cancel{2z}} \\\\ z+1 & =4z-(2z+1) \\\\ z+1 & =4z-2z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =4z-2z-1 \\\\ z+1 & =2z-1 \\\\ -z & =-2 \\\\ z & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250108 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250489 1069 "q-115792" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250489 1071 "Solve for $w$ where $1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w}=\\frac{w+5}{3w}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250489 1072 #uuid "c001b720-cfa8-54e2-8c25-eb8cf8283524" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250489 1074 "First, we note that $w=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $3w,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w} & =\\frac{w+5}{3w} \\\\ 3w\\cdot (1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w}) & =3w\\cdot (\\frac{w+5}{3w}) \\\\ 3w\\cdot 1-3w\\cdot \\frac{2w-3}{3w} & =3w\\cdot \\frac{w+5}{3w} \\\\ 3w-\\cancel{3w}\\cdot \\frac{2w-3}{\\cancel{3w}} & =\\cancel{3w}\\cdot \\frac{w+5}{\\cancel{3w}} \\\\ 3w-(2w-3) & =w+5 \\\\ 3w-2w+3 & =w+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $w,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w-2w+3 & =w+5 \\\\ w+3 & =w+5 \\\\ w-w & =5-3 \\\\ 0 & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a false statement. Therefore, we conclude that there is no solution." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249938 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250490 1069 "q-115794" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250490 1071 "Which value of $z$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250490 1072 #uuid "0a05ffdc-3b52-5cee-9869-369fd44c61d1" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250490 1074 "We note that $z=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3z$ and $6z.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3z,6z)=6z.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z} \\\\ 6z\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{6z}) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ 6z\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{6z}) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z})+6z\\cdot (\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ \\cancel{6z}\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3z}\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{\\cancel{3z}})+\\cancel{6z}\\cdot (\\frac{z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}) \\\\ 2z-1 & =2\\cdot (3z-1)+z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-1 & =2(3z-1)+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =6z-2+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =7z-3 \\\\ 2z-7z & =-3+1 \\\\ -5z & =-2 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=\\frac{2}{5}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249671 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250491 1069 "q-115810" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250491 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3-x}{4x-5}<0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250491 1072 #uuid "d9fbd632-5a91-5de9-9554-86c175581e67" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250491 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3-x}{4x-5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=3.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4}),\\,(\\frac{5}{4},3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})$ | $(\\frac{5}{4},3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $4x-5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})\\cup (3,\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250046 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250492 1069 "q-115813" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250492 1071 "![](images/5a/5a2a2b4b141f4f0c250e4865b174b9eb32b1ed4ab94cc6ebd074131673d5712c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-2{x}^{2}-4x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+2x+1$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}-1$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250492 1072 #uuid "3ef86548-9209-58c6-a39c-c5582ffe1c99" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250492 1074 "![](images/69/69465ce0573d7b6deac631663766a5dd1c90afa29eeb0c5286cb5ef1f7ee1153.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the line $x=-1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on this line.\n\n- Second, the line $x=-1$ divides the plane into $2$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $2$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2{x}^{2}-4x-2=-2(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+2x+1=(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-1=(x+1)(x-1)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249763 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250493 1069 "q-115832" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250493 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}+5}{(x+4)(3-x)}>0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250493 1072 #uuid "4f9744e7-a7a9-57af-bdae-6ed4c828c238" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250493 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+5}{(x+4)(3-x)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe equation $f(x)=0$ has no solution. On the other hand, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptotes at $x=-4$ and $x=3.$ These asymptotes divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-4),\\,(-4,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+5$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-4,3).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-4,3).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249863 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250494 1069 "q-115836" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250494 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2x+5}{({x}^{2}+3)(2-x)}>0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250494 1072 #uuid "08760f63-a3a6-5971-af6c-dce5f1758637" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250494 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2x+5}{({x}^{2}+3)(2-x)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=-\\frac{5}{2}.$ Also, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptote at $x=2.$  The root and the asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{2},2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{5}{2},2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+3$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive in the interval $(-\\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},2).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249747 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250495 1069 "q-115846" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250495 1071 "![](images/b6/b67f4648af37df7332a42a671fd8c3f7b0d7a8f626dc3ed9c4f32c1bb633f258.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250495 1072 #uuid "f3b70f9c-64c1-5ef5-826b-7d48f6c4267f" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250495 1074 "![](images/ef/ef7114aaf0f2cc275b79fe312ac6e99bda2bf07667eaa4ccfaae3920538381b5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}=2{x}^{2}(1-x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x=x(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249845 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250496 1069 "q-115850" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250496 1071 "![](images/b2/b2af712f3b4cca278137bd358d0f889b9b2a63b3ae3d54d3c699b974f43d3a06.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250496 1072 #uuid "bc2e2482-a37a-5def-9ca5-341d62ed5ca5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250496 1074 "![](images/00/0034c86bbb1f4412cba2a7d202170b87fcd2570789a37affb88b399b085b33f6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-1$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3=(x+1)(x-3)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2(x+1)(x-3).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $x=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $-1<x<3$ | $x>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250040 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250497 1069 "q-115852" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250497 1071 "![](images/3e/3e065edec23179a946ea28aa6f2b4f9a439ba23c014c38dded0cc7c756867a90.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250497 1072 #uuid "4093859b-39de-5aab-ae6c-e0a99b8d2362" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250497 1074 "![](images/5b/5b92aa0ecaf7dd906a39c44e31a12e725802d9c7c5cdc046351030ae04f6809e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=-1$ and $x=2.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x-2)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x+1)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$ \n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}$ become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}$ do *not* become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250107 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250498 1069 "q-115853" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250498 1071 "![](images/a3/a355d9b35a7ebf791a56116b0ee5b127d0dfd90d6958b5bebd04aa9ad09a9ddc.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250498 1072 #uuid "44061c57-e065-5be2-a07e-167019d4a0b2" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250498 1074 "![](images/62/6293d40f222d4d81236808f7ab0a35dfa82e1187b3a4d24bbc841a1b158f1039.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+x-2}=\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-2$ | $-2<x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250079 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250499 1069 "q-115854" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250499 1071 "![](images/75/753554f8129ab0ed83f19e5cadefba27cb3b700818bd3a3f801c64aaf01ded70.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250499 1072 #uuid "9285d6ff-fcc8-5fe1-ab97-90ac8bf0cd24" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250499 1074 "![](images/f2/f26e5b61c573664f0397667aff82cfe5d918e0d22f005658862c28c53d5de748.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=2$ and $x=4.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}=\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x-4)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(x-2)(x-4)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$ \n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}$ become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(1-\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}$ do *not* become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249794 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250500 1069 "q-115858" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250500 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{(2x+7)(3-x)}{{x}^{2}}\\le 0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250500 1072 #uuid "13d9ccfb-0e87-5a68-8e7f-a26501e83768" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250500 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(2x+7)(3-x)}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $x=3.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{7}{2},0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{7}{2},0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2})$ and $(3,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2}]\\cup [3,\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249706 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250501 1069 "q-115864" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250501 1071 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+11x>0?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250501 1072 #uuid "d9a349ab-e44f-5a1f-9f97-ccc234b5cc72" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250501 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+11x & >0 \\\\ x(x+11) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x+11)=0.$ The roots are $x=-11$ and $x=0$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-11),$ $(-11,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-11)$ | $(-11,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+11$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x+11)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(-)\\cdot (+)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x+11)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x+11)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-11)$ and $(0,\\infty ).$ \nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+11x>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-11)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250025 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250502 1069 "q-115871" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250502 1071 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-4x-12\\ge 0?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250502 1072 #uuid "afe6778b-3a23-52ed-bae8-c3f2da87f58d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250502 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x-12 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-6) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+2)(x-6)=0.$ The roots are $x=-2$ and $x=6,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-2,6),$ and $(6,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+2)(x-6)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+2)(x-6)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+2)(x-6)$ is zero o positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-2$ and $x=6.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-4x-12\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [6,\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250117 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250503 1069 "q-115876" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250503 1071 "![](images/be/bedcab6fac21c2c178604e94f04f31bf1f423562fd653fd18ae5e0ea949e38dc.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(y-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250503 1072 #uuid "d7681d42-68fd-5b0f-9442-7e7d7c7bcbfa" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250503 1074 "![](images/84/84b459d5682327d618116cd5d3ef331263548c754d2d8e0759e6fcb084fd466c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=-4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+2)(y+4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=-4,$ and $a+b=-6.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250035 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250504 1069 "q-115880" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250504 1071 "![](images/bc/bc5b29fca0521e3da39993d0359e857333611c68c35e49c037ca7af031288ba3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+4)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y+4)$\n- ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)(y+4)$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250504 1072 #uuid "335e4d6e-44ad-5f4c-9625-f1960ad24196" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250504 1074 "![](images/cd/cd73bc9f35e167537996a3b33b84471dcb8fefe2eccb4afd21ca55a23e6478fd.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y+4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)(y+4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=-4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=-1,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249910 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250505 1069 "q-115881" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250505 1071 "![](images/72/725cbaa4ce797e0436d1a53d4b1a9eedad5a15282fadfa8cb8f3381135e92d87.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250505 1072 #uuid "47ce069d-e0d7-5b9d-a02a-3975e0c397e3" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250505 1074 "The vertex of an upward-opening parabola is the lowest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/80/80f4e7505c204572ecbc3c2e1c207fa9f321bc9925f5e2e947db7feee45824f9.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(6,4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249928 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250506 1069 "q-115884" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250506 1071 "![](images/5f/5fbb2f7dac947b03c5ca28df14521e12c1ad6176531a0c3616412eca0469b619.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250506 1072 #uuid "d7877e40-366c-5f83-837b-ee9fd3315d35" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250506 1074 "The vertex of an upward-opening parabola is the lowest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/72/723357f6927823210c72f1bf93482506792abdac247224eb22eb0834e6ebd6ae.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(0,4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249808 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250507 1069 "q-115888" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250507 1071 "![](images/2e/2e2d4b78d8181efc77f65af0c3dc4b3110d0203e650006a0e99d9a8ba933b53f.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-(y-1)(y-4)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)(y-4)$\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y-1)$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250507 1072 #uuid "5780ba6c-1a2c-5799-b86a-8148f7437eba" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250507 1074 "![](images/7e/7e207bc990a9dfbafa8474ad13c17482e21c323833be848f4ebbaa580abeb201.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=1$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-(y-1)(y-4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)(y-4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250009 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250508 1069 "q-115889" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250508 1071 "![](images/d0/d01bb927b221e0f0c55009cdcbbdf8588833c50a6ec637f2e34b85b683426c1e.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250508 1072 #uuid "b2fd30fd-8dc3-5bfa-b114-30a9f20fc257" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250508 1074 "The vertex of a downward-opening parabola is the highest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/9e/9e73d4e1d8cc8a61fffeab716359b29b31176f9669eff852d9d7817ccd4e8ef5.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(-4,-4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249937 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250509 1069 "q-115891" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250509 1071 "![](images/bc/bc32c3b428a2e649c5e9ff4f9425c40cdb41ba65972953c2e1a419526878dde6.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250509 1072 #uuid "5ec60edb-0bc5-56ff-8c1e-21f9f1162a2d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250509 1074 "The vertex of a right-opening parabola is the left-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/cd/cd9376f67191ac00f58fa74a066d69008b2d43e6743af62ee6577911374ff5eb.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(6,4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249709 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250510 1069 "q-115898" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250510 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=6y?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250510 1072 #uuid "8a80168e-4de0-55ab-b42f-115076b5300e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250510 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(\\frac{3}{2})y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249919 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250511 1069 "q-115905" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250511 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=2x?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250511 1072 #uuid "7b53a4d3-8dde-5b34-8af3-3a4b04e9f45b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250511 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4(\\frac{1}{2})x.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249847 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250512 1069 "q-115906" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250512 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-12x?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250512 1072 #uuid "e64ad999-d45e-5953-8ff8-93083e4cff0c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250512 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4(-3)x.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-3.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249748 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250513 1069 "q-115908" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250513 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-32x?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250513 1072 #uuid "9183fe81-7ce3-5d95-8bc2-6c6807cc4856" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250513 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4(-8)x.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-8.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249883 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250514 1069 "q-115911" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250514 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(y-1)^{2}=5(x+7)?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250514 1072 #uuid "9ec04397-a1d2-5a43-be90-9c3301fd7dc0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250514 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4(\\frac{5}{4})(x+7).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-7$ and $k=1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-7,1).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250074 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250515 1069 "q-115919" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250515 1071 "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to three-fifths of the rate of increase of the diameter. Determine the radius of the circle at that moment." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250515 1072 #uuid "27f3a217-c646-5edd-93b0-a8b079b9f019" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250515 1074 "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{D}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250098 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250516 1069 "q-115931" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250516 1071 "![](images/4f/4f9f16654bc92f01c9cda8308ab4679695ed7d5055a12f6963303d4071c95bea.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250516 1072 #uuid "227a73c6-003b-5008-b8f1-e0d13c23cc66" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250516 1074 "![](images/02/0234179c0a2dfb514a921f19e6fdc7bcad022aa0368d122a7d2ecc0e5d27df73.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}(2-y) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(2-y) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250030 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250517 1069 "q-115935" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250517 1071 "![](images/57/57ecd122ba41b296b3884ecd4f748f6b4ac4bd1d26f14d7b5166c5fe6e33674b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(1-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250517 1072 #uuid "d530f455-01e1-528c-b25a-48e2a9adee74" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250517 1074 "![](images/e1/e109d237419aaba5efcc0c65c2d97c8f8ded17d18f98bb0a95eba3336d224590.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=\\pm 1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(1-y)$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250077 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250518 1069 "q-115940" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250518 1071 "![](images/92/92c557774a625d93c34fea181f435c1e6e544daa606a76c512bd96a7b9c047e7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(3-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-3)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250518 1072 #uuid "fe403345-e284-5ce7-b15f-086966b32179" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250518 1074 "![](images/58/583fe0e13218462bdb4286ce456b0bcc4d43dd3772033e62a53242107a0a69db.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(3-y)$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-3)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=3,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249930 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250519 1069 "q-115941" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250519 1071 "![](images/9b/9be53ddfc5bd7a471ced46b76f6c8bb78fbc8b6831cec1689b6d98e8561c0d87.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250519 1072 #uuid "6d344ae1-e6c6-5690-b475-f3d97d5d4a83" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250519 1074 "![](images/50/50b53f35a936263f72a3962e32d841dc85519fb5ef3e7e138111c4efeb38137e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y+1)(2-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=y(y+1)(2-y).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250047 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250520 1069 "q-115951" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250520 1071 "Consider the curve ${e}^{x}-{e}^{y}=y.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3$ on the curve at the point where $y=0,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250520 1072 #uuid "32cbcf42-bec0-509f-ae29-5197f19f2be6" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250520 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & =y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x}-{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{x}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({e}^{y}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3$ and $y=0.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)\\cdot {e}^{x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({e}^{0}+1) \\\\ -3{e}^{x} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=0$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x}-{e}^{0} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}-1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{0} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -3 & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250120 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250521 1069 "q-115978" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250521 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}-5x}<0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250521 1072 #uuid "16c023ab-829a-5e40-b454-d582f297e7f9" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250521 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}-5x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{x(x-5)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)=0$ has no solution. On the other hand, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=0$ and $x=5.$ These asymptotes divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0),\\,(0,5),\\,(5,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(0,5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (0,5).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250000 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250522 1069 "q-115979" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250522 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{4-x}\\ge 0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250522 1072 #uuid "2ca0e23e-00c3-5452-8761-187b7954262c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250522 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{4-x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+1)}{4-x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=0.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=4.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $4-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(0,4).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249811 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250523 1069 "q-115985" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250523 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x+5}\\ge 0.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250523 1072 #uuid "17c18325-c888-5d0f-936c-fd5cb7917a52" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250523 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-5.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-2),\\,(-2,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-5,-2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-5,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249985 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250524 1069 "q-116040" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250524 1071 "![](images/dd/ddb340a99b8ab92d60e17184274854ce3d1b1bfa4972a79e74b8bfe9b0a81ce7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+3}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+3)^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{y+3}$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250524 1072 #uuid "df71ed63-f106-5382-97b4-fb9c0eee25d2" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250524 1074 "![](images/73/73ba54766cd08a0b596e43cd8da93209e2548c68821e70a44418a24dfdf27a38.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -3.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y<-3$ and negative for $y>-3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+3}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to -3.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y<-3$ and negative for $y>-3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{(y+3)^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for all $y\\ne -3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{y+3}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{y}{y+3}=-\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{3}{y})}\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\infty .$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249693 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250525 1069 "q-116073" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250525 1071 "For which of the following graphs would the trapezoidal rule give an overestimate of the area under the curve between $x=1$ and $x=4?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250525 1072 #uuid "5531051c-1e07-5080-88c8-4721e0df0982" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250525 1074 "The trapezoidal rule overestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave up over the given interval.    \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave up on $[1,4]$ is the one shown below.\n\n![](images/49/495c2b8658aa5d06d22a9bf9237bb942b35dc72bb5baa205f5d464985d87f87c.png)" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249844 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250526 1069 "q-116081" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250526 1071 "For which of the following graphs would the trapezoidal rule give an overestimate of the area under the curve between $x=0$ and $x=3?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250526 1072 #uuid "6c930d52-b7c8-5e5b-bc31-d5abd7667716" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250526 1074 "The trapezoidal rule overestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave up over the given interval.    \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave up on $[0,3]$ is the one shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf44f59745d8fefd48ed9106bbfcbc7e59eba1df0cf0e49fdcb6ca3e9c5a1db6.png)" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249692 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250527 1069 "q-116082" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250527 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{6}=1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250527 1072 #uuid "90b7b991-4df4-5fbc-845c-9b5e171cb456" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250527 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=-2$ and $k=4.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-2,4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250053 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250528 1069 "q-116086" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250528 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y-8)^{2}}{4}=1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250528 1072 #uuid "7e81ea0d-5dc3-5357-b544-eddb497dca4a" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250528 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-8)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=2$ and $k=8.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(2,8).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249678 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250529 1069 "q-116093" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250529 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}=1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250529 1072 #uuid "17dd6de7-f230-5537-90dc-a34c85d5215c" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250529 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-2$ and $k=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-2,-1).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250003 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250530 1069 "q-116100" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250530 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}=1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250530 1072 #uuid "35bb8fc9-b273-5104-bbf5-373700e22296" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250530 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-1$ and $k=5.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-1,5).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249891 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250531 1069 "q-116102" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250531 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}=1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250531 1072 #uuid "61246893-8862-5ada-b3e6-46dc520143df" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250531 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=0$ and $k=4.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(0,4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249856 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250532 1069 "q-116103" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250532 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{36}=1?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250532 1072 #uuid "b2a54ae2-f488-56eb-9b24-9f9d28627e54" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250532 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-(-4))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-6))^{2}}{{6}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-6$ and $k=-4.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-6,-4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249683 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250533 1069 "q-116104" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250533 1071 "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250533 1072 #uuid "0f403d57-f6d2-5901-9ad6-4def1edb7127" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250533 1074 "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $x$ and ${x}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,{x}^{2})={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${x}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x})-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}) & =x\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\cancel{x}})-\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{x-3}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}) \\\\ (3x-1) & =x\\cdot 2-(x-3) \\\\ (3x-1) & =2x-(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-1) & =2x-(x-3) \\\\ 3x-1 & =2x-x+3 \\\\ 3x-1 & =x+3 \\\\ 3x-x & =3+1 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249843 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250534 1069 "q-116106" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250534 1071 "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250534 1072 #uuid "3783baa6-61fe-5e5f-a6e5-bd47f3137af5" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250534 1074 "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $2{m}^{2},$ ${m}^{2}$ and $m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m,{m}^{2},2{m}^{2})=2{m}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2{m}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}} \\\\ 2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}) & =2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}) \\\\ 2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m})-2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}) & =2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}) \\\\ 2m\\cdot \\cancel{m}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{m}})-\\cancel{2{m}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{3m+4}{\\cancel{2{m}^{2}}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{{m}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{m}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2m\\cdot 3-(3m+4) & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6m-(3m+4) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6m-(3m+4) & =2 \\\\ 6m-3m-4 & =2 \\\\ 3m & =6 \\\\ m & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249951 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250535 1069 "q-116107" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250535 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola \n${x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}+6x+48y=71?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250535 1072 #uuid "7f27bda9-78fa-5fec-acdb-71588b2abb59" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250535 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}+6x+48y & =71 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x-8{y}^{2}+48y & =71 \\\\ [{x}^{2}+6x]-8[{y}^{2}-6y] & =71 \\\\ [(x+3)^{2}-9]-8[(y-3)^{2}-9] & =71 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}-9-8(y-3)^{2}+72 & =71 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}-8(y-3)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{8}-(y-3)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-3))^{2}}{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(-3,3).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249710 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250536 1069 "q-116110" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250536 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$6{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}+48x-48y=72?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250536 1072 #uuid "9369b477-cb7c-5149-8c85-5d42daef382f" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250536 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}+48x-48y & =72 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+48x-4{y}^{2}-48y & =72 \\\\ 6[{x}^{2}+8x]-4[{y}^{2}+12y] & =72 \\\\ 6[(x+4)^{2}-16]-4[(y+6)^{2}-36] & =72 \\\\ 6(x+4)^{2}-96-4(y+6)^{2}+144 & =72 \\\\ 6(x+4)^{2}-4(y+6)^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-(-6))^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(-4,-6).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249908 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250537 1069 "q-116113" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250537 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}-40x+6y=-85?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250537 1072 #uuid "55d78d11-140f-535d-9e65-7b7af7dfd48e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250537 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}-40x+6y & =-85 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-40x-3{y}^{2}+6y & =-85 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-10x]-3[{y}^{2}-2y] & =-85 \\\\ 4[(x-5)^{2}-25]-3[(y-1)^{2}-1] & =-85 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}-100-3(y-1)^{2}+3 & =-85 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}-3(y-1)^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(5,1).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249804 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250538 1069 "q-116116" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250538 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $6{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}-60y+8x=-122?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250538 1072 #uuid "09ea155c-8ce4-5cf1-a51e-cc7b1072dc21" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250538 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}-60y+8x & =-122 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}-60y-4{x}^{2}+8x & =-122 \\\\ 6[{y}^{2}-10y]-4[{x}^{2}-2x] & =-122 \\\\ 6[(y-5)^{2}-25]-4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-122 \\\\ 6(y-5)^{2}-150-4(x-1)^{2}+4 & =-122 \\\\ 6(y-5)^{2}-4(x-1)^{2} & =-122+150-4 \\\\ 6(y-5)^{2}-4(x-1)^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(1,5).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250093 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250539 1069 "q-116118" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250539 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$-{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+12x+24y=-9?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250539 1072 #uuid "a90f2a56-a098-5b2a-9f22-92ea871fa219" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250539 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+12x+24y & =-9 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}+24y-{x}^{2}+12x & =-9 \\\\ 3[{y}^{2}+8y]-[{x}^{2}-12x] & =-9 \\\\ 3[(y+4)^{2}-16]-[(x-6)^{2}-36] & =-9 \\\\ 3(y+4)^{2}-48-(x-6)^{2}+36 & =-9 \\\\ 3(y+4)^{2}-(x-6)^{2} & =-9+48-36 \\\\ 3(y+4)^{2}-(x-6)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (y+4)^{2}-\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-(-4))^{2}}{{1}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(6,-4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249848 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250540 1069 "q-116122" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250540 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$-3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-24x+8y=41?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250540 1072 #uuid "9ec88459-3130-5f14-825f-431ad11ade33" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250540 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-24x+8y & =41 \\\\ {y}^{2}+8y-3{x}^{2}-24x & =41 \\\\ [{y}^{2}+8y]-3[{x}^{2}+8x] & =41 \\\\ [(y+4)^{2}-16]-3[(x+4)^{2}-16] & =41 \\\\ (y+4)^{2}-16-3(x+4)^{2}+48 & =41 \\\\ (y+4)^{2}-3(x+4)^{2} & =41+16-48 \\\\ (y+4)^{2}-3(x+4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-(-4))^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(-4,-4).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249762 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250541 1069 "q-116123" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250541 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-10x+4y=31?$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250541 1072 #uuid "c70eacd9-b18b-5a32-b727-b9fb2382ee5d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250541 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-10x+4y & =31 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+4y-{x}^{2}-10x & =31 \\\\ 2[{y}^{2}+2y]-[{x}^{2}+10x] & =31 \\\\ 2[(y+1)^{2}-1]-[(x+5)^{2}-25] & =31 \\\\ 2(y+1)^{2}-2-(x+5)^{2}+25 & =31 \\\\ 2(y+1)^{2}-(x+5)^{2} & =31+2-25 \\\\ 2(y+1)^{2}-(x+5)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-5))^{2}}{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(-5,-1).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250109 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250542 1069 "q-116150" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250542 1071 "![](images/15/158e4f6067ce12d5a31cc6ee0b217a8d11a459db5883fdd0b28ffdc2bd969a5e.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}+4{y}^{2}+4y$\n- ${y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250542 1072 #uuid "b5fe91b2-7a65-5a6c-99ca-fdba41d5ec25" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250542 1074 "![](images/41/41b6dc7fd4e9a7dba6e14fe2d9fb87e7c07736ec0fc3cc111dd347b61c1075bb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}=-{y}^{2}(y+2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+4{y}^{2}+4y=y({y}^{2}+4y+4)=y(y+2)^{2}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}=y(2-y)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250065 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250543 1069 "q-116174" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250543 1071 "![](images/ee/eeacce39a60a75fd71a77d54f0008f4469395d5e9b69912db8ce9896320b667f.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250543 1072 #uuid "cd378446-2d55-575f-ba05-b037c821e22e" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250543 1074 "![](images/db/dbb1d802854d4046479c69d297fe1807a9ded488409b385a6a2cffbf1196b4c4.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y=-y({y}^{2}-6y+9)=-y(y-3)^{2}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-3y=y(y-3).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y=-y(y-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249989 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250544 1069 "q-116177" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250544 1071 "![](images/8f/8f023d50d11e5b6604e5fcc257e35aa4b8615e8c91f5855d10e73293720c27e4.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250544 1072 #uuid "930070fc-32d3-575b-8b2d-70f53ae4fe30" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250544 1074 "![](images/b6/b6cf036f732d1cdff6f2e575b2ce082979d986416afd195282789303dcf70131.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}$ become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ do *not* become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250081 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250545 1069 "q-116178" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250545 1071 "![](images/9e/9e368467f35af6f9f8c13dcfb2d8edc2547e2ab123b4bd2bfd68e6878cea9907.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250545 1072 #uuid "383f6890-3363-5870-ad1d-d5dde2132ca3" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250545 1074 "![](images/63/632d27d8354cdde40f59894fc017c49a49d47003d2f914ed0deef8c6751d7c1d.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to -2$ only. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{(y+2)^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{y+2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{3}{y+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $y\\to \\infty .$\n\nOf the remaining options, the only two that give this behavior are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{(y+2)^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{3}{y+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, the slopes of the slope field are positive for all $y\\ne -2.$ \n\nTherefore, the equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{y+2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y>-2.$\n\nOn the other hand, the equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+2)^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that the right-hand side of this equation is positive for all $y\\ne -2.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+2)^{2}}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249862 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250546 1069 "q-116179" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250546 1071 "![](images/7b/7b3aa65a62cb70ae297b57c5cfceecec8035afb621acec93d6fceabd1fd48729.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250546 1072 #uuid "5fd03aa6-eac8-5663-bd24-fcfbb33f2563" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250546 1074 "![](images/89/890640809739a95a6d068f65678e124cc92d9e6d2fd86ec0380ee1f37a1c56d1.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 2$ only. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2-y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{(y-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the line $y=2$ divides the plane into $2$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $2$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-y}.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249783 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250547 1069 "q-116180" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250547 1071 "![](images/6d/6d0a816057423c4f5397f876f775b0431eac550bb1a02d4e4f7021c33c37809e.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250547 1072 #uuid "8c6d2a4b-5b8c-5a21-b9fd-3894bc930dc9" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250547 1074 "![](images/f3/f3b0dad3f1cb4b2216e3fce7f1fefa8beac15a6e16ab09ab3a4c47a2472e2f9a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=-3$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}={y}^{3}(y+3)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}={y}^{2}(y+3).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-3$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-3$ | $-3<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}={y}^{3}(y+3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249782 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250548 1069 "q-116189" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250548 1071 "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250548 1072 #uuid "7d95fb74-c417-52e3-a597-8d02622d9069" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250548 1074 "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2} & \\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x-\\frac{35}{2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+2x-35) & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x+7)(x-5) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+7)(x-5) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+7)(x-5).$ The roots are $x=-7$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ $(-7,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+7)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+7)(x-5)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+7)(x-5)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-7)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-7$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [5,\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249971 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250549 1069 "q-116190" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250549 1071 "Find the solution of $\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+2x\\le x+6.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250549 1072 #uuid "20fb18bd-bfa4-5326-a8c9-dd6b494b5b5b" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250549 1074 "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+2x & \\le x+6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+3x-18) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+6)(x-3) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+6)(x-3) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+6)(x-3)=0.$ The roots are $x=-6$ and $x=3,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-6),$ $(-6,3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-6)$ | $(-6,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+6$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+6)(x-3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+6)(x-3)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+6)(x-3)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-6,3).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-6$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+2x\\le x+6$ for all $x\\in [-6,3].$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249994 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250550 1069 "q-116194" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250550 1071 "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 4-8x-{x}^{2}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250550 1072 #uuid "70d44166-fe43-573d-87ca-1e5612abc3bd" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250550 1074 "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x & \\ge 4-8x-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+7x-4 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+4)(2x-1) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+4)(2x-1).$ The roots are $x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-4),$ $(-4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+4)(2x-1)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+4)(2x-1)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+4)(2x-1)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 4-8x-{x}^{2}$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250033 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250551 1069 "q-116200" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250551 1071 "Find the solution of $\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}x\\ge 2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250551 1072 #uuid "f895ed60-2cc3-5f28-b4ed-ecac286a96ee" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250551 1074 "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}x & \\ge 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}x-2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}({x}^{2}+3x-10) & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(x+5)(x-2) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+5)(x-2) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+5)(x-2).$ The roots are $x=-5$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-5,2),$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+5)(x-2)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+5)(x-2)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+5)(x-2)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-5$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}x\\ge 2$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250017 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250552 1069 "q-116221" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250552 1071 "Solve the inequality $4(1-x)^{6}-1>7.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250552 1072 #uuid "41cd9f4f-608d-5b7a-af38-8597aa9cfdcc" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250552 1074 "Isolating the sixth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1-x)^{6}-1 & >7 \\\\ 4(1-x)^{6} & >8 \\\\ (1-x)^{6} & >2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the sixth root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[6]{(1-x)^{6}} & >\\sqrt[6]{2} \\\\ |1-x| & >\\sqrt[6]{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n1-x<-\\sqrt[6]{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,1-x>\\sqrt[6]{2}\n$$\n\nWe can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-x & <-\\sqrt[6]{2} &  &  & 1-x & >\\sqrt[6]{2} \\\\ -x & <-\\sqrt[6]{2}-1 &  &  & -x & >\\sqrt[6]{2}-1 \\\\ x & >1+\\sqrt[6]{2} &  &  & x & <1-\\sqrt[6]{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is\n\n$$\nx<1-\\sqrt[6]{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>1+\\sqrt[6]{2}.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249947 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250553 1069 "q-116224" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250553 1071 "Solve the inequality $2(x+4)^{4}+6<18.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250553 1072 #uuid "1df278ab-6bd5-5ff2-ae79-79e5ce828677" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250553 1074 "Isolating the quartic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)^{4}+6 & <18 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{4} & <12 \\\\ (x+4)^{4} & <6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the fourth root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{(x+4)^{4}} & <\\sqrt[4]{6} \\\\ |x+4| & <\\sqrt[4]{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt[4]{6}<x+4<\\sqrt[4]{6}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt[4]{6}<x+4 & <\\sqrt[4]{6} \\\\ -\\sqrt[4]{6}-4<x & <\\sqrt[4]{6}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249957 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250554 1069 "q-116229" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250554 1071 "Solve $(4x-5)^{4}+25\\le 9.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250554 1072 #uuid "fff2036c-c0ee-5a4f-b443-05403b1769e0" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250554 1074 "Isolating the quartic term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4x-5)^{4}+25 & \\le 9 \\\\ (4x-5)^{4} & \\le -16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $(4x-5)^{4}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x$ because the power $(4)$ is even.\n\nTherefore, there is no solution to the given inequality." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250118 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250555 1069 "q-116241" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250555 1071 "Solve $2(2-5x)^{8}+7<3.$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250555 1072 #uuid "4e379fb5-67ad-5658-ac6d-11896f892169" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250555 1074 "Isolating the eighth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2-5x)^{8}+7 & <3 \\\\ 2(2-5x)^{8} & <-4 \\\\ (2-5x)^{8} & <-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $(2-5x)^{8}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x$ because the power $(8)$ is even.\n\nTherefore, there is no solution to the given inequality." 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249961 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250556 1069 "q-116263" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250556 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))(4x+7)\\le 0.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250556 1072 #uuid "e09f9e03-11b8-591d-ad7a-74504a51c73d" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250556 1074 "Since $-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2)$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))(4x+7) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))(4x+7)}{(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))} & \\ge \\frac{0}{(-1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+2))} \\\\ 4x+7 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge -7 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249793 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250557 1069 "q-116265" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250557 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9)<0.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250557 1072 #uuid "00c3c871-2f91-5013-a7bd-a1de3fb8e0eb" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true] [17592186250557 1074 "Note that the factor $2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi $ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⇒\\,-4\\pi <2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi <-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince $2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi $ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi )(2x+9)}{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi } & >\\frac{0}{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+3)-3\\pi } \\\\ 2x+9 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >-9 \\\\ x & >-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-001d516e-ba06-5b95-b4a6-f35dab9a3672" 17592186247421 "answer-00a986a9-dc99-5796-9957-e44b0b8d9b54" 17592186247422 "answer-00bb67d8-490a-5026-b8f6-d6d24d823e00" 17592186247423 "answer-00c187d3-7a69-54ea-bf51-0b58dc71093a" 17592186247424 "answer-00c4a36b-1ea2-5c73-8a8e-897727eb8f12" 17592186247425 "answer-01083aba-7159-5668-975e-80fd900b5e13" 17592186247426 "answer-011694ea-b18f-5bb8-b2aa-59cb077d8853" 17592186247427 "answer-01229e17-29dc-5c11-a1d0-77f98e694104" 17592186247428 "answer-012323fc-4e7b-5948-bd33-ac6535cdacea" 17592186247429 "answer-0125f9b6-2322-54f8-9097-2bc2a02cc5fe" 17592186247430 "answer-015beb6d-72d8-594f-b713-3636e887cac3" 17592186247431 "answer-016f5ab0-617f-59ac-a937-26f6c7b40214" 17592186247432 "answer-01764684-3b80-58ae-8acb-d199ca383b29" 17592186247433 "answer-01827748-212d-560e-98f3-b45c7c8273e7" 17592186247434 "answer-0188c738-8d3f-5f42-b61d-b6905e5bfdd7" 17592186247435 "answer-018d8348-3190-5eea-b417-c63541e4aff5" 17592186247436 "answer-019828a0-fbdc-537a-8fe9-5b80de591075" 17592186247437 "answer-01a0e5e1-b1da-5dc9-b41e-3bb393e24954" 17592186247438 "answer-01b3e267-b1b1-5bb0-8872-5e95d4626af4" 17592186247439 "answer-01b573ef-1007-53ad-a1f4-f1bbb6a97f3a" 17592186247440 "answer-01c50259-96ff-5091-b6d8-d010aabcd87a" 17592186247441 "answer-01fdb005-8179-54c2-a098-7a92883a30b5" 17592186247442 "answer-022d7b7b-070a-51f5-bd49-94b4db34c49a" 17592186247443 "answer-0237b559-7d33-5cd0-